00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:54.360
Let's talk about fitness and let's talk

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.340
about how you can develop
the optimal fitness

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:57.500
protocols for you.

00:18:57.500 --> 00:19:00.390
So that includes what to
do each day of the week

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:02.610
and your fitness protocol across the week,

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:04.680
and indeed across the month and the year

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:06.630
and even year to year.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:09.120
When we had Dr. Andy
Galpin on the podcast,

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.160
he said something very
important that we want to keep

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:15.660
in mind today, which is concepts
are few, methods are many,

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:18.285
that is there are an
infinite number of different

00:19:18.285 --> 00:19:21.300
programs and exercises
and set and rep schemes

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.540
and different runs and burpees
and pushups, et cetera,

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:26.040
et cetera that one can follow.

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:30.120
However, there are really
just a few basic concepts

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.875
or principles of muscle physiology,

00:19:32.875 --> 00:19:35.880
of cardiovascular function,
of connective tissue

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.760
function that provide or set the basis

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:41.700
for the adaptations that we call fitness

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:43.170
or that lead to fitness.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.120
So I'm going to list those off now.

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:47.850
We can talk about a fitness
protocol that's really

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.610
aimed mainly toward developing skill.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:52.740
That's one. Or speed.

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:53.880
That's another.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:57.120
Or power, which is speed times strength,

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:00.720
or specifically strength, or hypertrophy,

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:04.800
growth of muscles, or endurance
such as muscular endurance.

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:06.480
Muscular endurance is, for instance,

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:10.080
your ability to stay in
a plank position or to do

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:14.640
a wall sit, to sit on an
invisible chair against a wall,

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.580
or other forms of endurance like near

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.020
pure anaerobic endurance.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:23.370
So a one minute sprint or
less or a one minute all

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:27.000
out cycling on stationary
bike, this sort of thing,

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.690
or endurance that occurs
in the kind of 3 to 12

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:33.180
minute total duration range.

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:36.510
So that might be sprints or high intensity

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:37.830
interval type training.

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:41.850
It could be an all out swim,
it could be all out row.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:43.080
That's another form of endurance,

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:44.850
taps into different fuel systems,

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:47.727
different aspects of muscle
physiology, et cetera.

00:20:47.727 --> 00:20:50.730
And then endurance that
lasts 30 minutes or more,

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:52.050
which is typically what people think

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:53.790
about when they think about endurance.

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:55.650
But of course, the other
forms of endurance matter.

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:59.070
So we've got skill, speed,
power, strength, hypertrophy,

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:02.313
muscular endurance, anaerobic endurance,

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:05.610
what I would call 3 to
12 minute endurance,

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.560
although it goes by other names as well,

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:09.641
and 30 minutes or more endurance type

00:21:09.641 --> 00:21:12.540
exercise and adaptations.

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:16.470
Each and every one of these
requires different principles,

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:20.100
different concepts in order to improve,

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:23.340
say your muscular strength
or your hypertrophy or both.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:26.550
However, there's a general
theme that sits beneath

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:29.280
all adaptations leading to fitness,

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:30.990
and that's what we're
really going to set down

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:32.220
as the base layer,

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:34.560
the foundation of everything
we talk about today.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:38.250
And that's that we need
to think about what are

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:39.810
the modifiable variables?

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.030
Again, I'm borrowing
directly from the episode

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:43.050
with Dr. Andy Galpin.

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:43.883
He was the one that said,

00:21:43.883 --> 00:21:46.320
modifiable variables are the
key thing to think about.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:47.550
What are you going to modify?

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.744
What are you going to change
in order to increase one

00:21:50.744 --> 00:21:55.744
or some of the various things
I listed off before, skill,

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:57.900
speed, power, strength,
hypertrophy, endurance,

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.310
et cetera, et cetera.

00:21:59.310 --> 00:22:02.130
And some of the key concepts that emerge

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:04.020
from that discussion are
that we need to think

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:05.460
about progressive overload.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.320
Normally when people hear
about progressive overload,

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:09.570
they think about adding more
weight to a bar or picking

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:12.360
up heavier dumbbells,
but that could also be

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:14.460
progressive overload in
the context of running up

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:16.860
a hill of steeper incline
or running a little bit

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:19.508
faster or a little bit further
and so on and so forth.

00:22:19.508 --> 00:22:21.030
Now, as I promised earlier,

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:23.430
today we are not going to
drill into each and every one

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:25.440
of the mechanisms that
underlie the different

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:28.170
adaptations that are
going to develop speed

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:30.060
and strength and endurance, et cetera,

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:31.560
because that was covered in the podcast

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:33.388
with Dr. Andy Galpin and the other podcast

00:22:33.388 --> 00:22:35.370
with experts that I mentioned earlier.

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:37.410
And we again, will provide
links to those podcasts

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:39.750
if you want to drill
into those mechanisms.

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:42.300
Instead, what we are going
to do is we're going to start

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.900
with a program that
essentially is designed

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:49.740
for you to maximize all aspects of fitness

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:52.800
to the extent that you can
simultaneously maximize

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.300
all aspects of fitness,

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:57.960
but then to change or modify that protocol

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:00.540
so that if you want to
build up more, for instance,

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:02.910
strength and you want to
just hold onto the endurance

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:05.670
you have, you don't
want to build endurance,

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:08.760
at least not in that week or
that month, you can do that.

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:10.020
Or if you want to improve your endurance

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:12.750
while maintaining your
strength, you can do that.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:14.730
And so on and so forth.

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:16.290
Most people, I do believe,

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:19.860
would like a combination
of strength and endurance

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:22.500
and flexibility and
maybe even hypertrophy,

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:25.620
particularly for certain
muscle groups that maybe are

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:27.360
not as well developed
as other muscle groups.

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:29.100
They want to bring
balance to their physique,

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:31.224
both for sake of aesthetics
and for sake of health

00:23:31.224 --> 00:23:34.320
and for sake of general functioning,

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:36.390
to maybe even to eliminate pain,

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:38.850
the protocol that I'm going
to describe really works

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.430
as a foundational
template for that as well.

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:43.920
So let's drill into that
foundational protocol

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:47.250
and I'll keep referring to it
as the foundational protocol,

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:48.600
not because it's the one that I use,

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:49.797
although it is the one that I use,

00:23:49.797 --> 00:23:51.360
and not because it's the
one that we're talking

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.890
about today, although
it's the one we're talking

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:54.930
about today, but because
we need some general

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:57.210
framework from which to build out the more

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:59.250
specific protocols that
we'll get into in a bit

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:00.720
more detail later.

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:02.880
So in this foundational
protocol for fitness,

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:06.240
what you'll notice is
that on any one given day,

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:09.360
you're going to focus on one
particular aspect of fitness.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:11.130
Maybe it's endurance, maybe it's strength,

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:13.590
maybe it's hypertrophy, in particular,

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.750
it might be hypertrophy for
a particular muscle group

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.430
or muscle groups.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:20.100
That said, across the entire week,

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:22.890
it's designed to bring
fitness and different forms

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:26.490
of fitness to all aspects of your body.

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:28.704
So this particular protocol begins

00:24:28.704 --> 00:24:32.550
on Sunday, although that's
simply the day that I happen

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:33.720
to begin the protocol.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:36.120
And again, this protocol is not important

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:37.301
because it's the one that I follow.

00:24:37.301 --> 00:24:39.419
I follow it because it is important.

00:24:39.419 --> 00:24:42.150
In other words, it's a
protocol that's really gleaned

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:43.991
from the scientific
literature and the experts,

00:24:43.991 --> 00:24:45.120
that is for you.

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:47.160
So this fitness protocol
is really about you.

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:50.100
I just may refer to it as
the one that I follow simply

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:51.360
for ease of communication.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:53.820
And for me, my week begins on Sunday.

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:58.080
So I do my very best to
get a workout in on Sunday.

00:24:58.080 --> 00:25:02.280
And for me, that workout is
that of a endurance workout.

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:07.280
It's designed to either maintain
or increase my endurance,

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:09.600
and the endurance type that I'm referring

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:11.820
to is endurance of 30 minutes or more.

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:16.630
In fact, for me, the goal
is always to get either 60

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:18.900
to 75 minutes of jogging.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:21.690
So this would be
so-called zone two cardio,

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:23.700
people probably have
heard of zone two cardio,

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:24.750
but if you haven't, that's okay.

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:27.360
Zone two cardio is something
that you could measure

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:29.370
with a heart rate monitor or other device,

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.050
but you don't need to.

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:33.591
Zone two cardio is the kind
of cardiovascular exercise

00:25:33.591 --> 00:25:37.874
in which you're pushing yourself to move

00:25:37.874 --> 00:25:41.400
such that you're breathing
faster than normal,

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:43.860
your heart is beating
faster than normal, however,

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:46.560
you are still able to
sustain a conversation.

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:50.010
But if you were to push
yourself any harder,

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:52.890
that is move faster or
go up a steeper incline

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:55.260
at the same rate you happen
to be at any one moment,

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:57.660
you would lose that ability to speak,

00:25:57.660 --> 00:25:59.040
you wouldn't be able
to complete sentences,

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:00.360
you would be out of breath or you'd have

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:01.830
to pause mid-sentence.

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:04.620
Now, it's near impossible,
even with a heart rate monitor,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.920
to stay exactly in zone
two unless you're very,

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:08.760
very skilled at that.

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:10.350
So I don't obsess over that,

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:12.000
and in fact I don't wear
a heart rate monitor

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:13.585
when I do this exercise, but for me,

00:26:13.585 --> 00:26:16.980
the goal is to head out
on Sunday and get 60 to 75

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:19.830
minutes of jogging in zone two.

00:26:19.830 --> 00:26:21.177
Now of course I like to jog,

00:26:21.177 --> 00:26:22.770
but that doesn't mean
that you have to jog.

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:27.000
You could replace jogging with
rowing on a rowing machine

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.710
or maybe even rowing an actual boat

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.765
if you have access to that
or cycling or swimming,

00:26:31.765 --> 00:26:34.340
something that allows you
continuous movement for 60

00:26:34.340 --> 00:26:38.670
to 75 minutes at that zone two threshold

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:40.680
we talked about earlier.

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:43.920
For me, that can include some
hills, and when I say hills,

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:44.970
they could be very steep hills,

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:48.111
but I simply slow my pace
down in order to stay

00:26:48.111 --> 00:26:51.390
in that roughly zone two range.

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:55.140
Or it could be that they
are more low grade hills

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:57.420
and I might just slow down
a little bit or I might

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:59.460
even push myself a tiny bit that day.

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:01.830
But really I'm just trying
to build that long endurance.

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:03.840
I'm trying to build up
my capacity or maintain

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:07.500
my capacity to go a long
distance without fatiguing.

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.890
Now some days, meaning
some Sundays, since I tend

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:12.150
to do this almost always

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:14.130
on Sunday, although there are exceptions,

00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:16.450
instead of doing the 60 to 75 minute jog,

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:20.160
what I'll do is I will
head out for a long hike

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:22.380
that could be two and a
half hours or three hours

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:24.270
or maybe even a four or five hour hike.

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:25.890
Sometimes it's very long.

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:30.630
And I'll do that sometimes
simply to mix up the routine,

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:33.187
because sometimes jogging
and jogging the same routes

00:27:33.187 --> 00:27:34.710
gets boring to me.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.000
I do enjoy running,

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:38.070
that's something I've been
doing for a very long time,

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:41.940
but sometimes it just
gets a little bit tedious

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:43.238
and I want to do something different.

00:27:43.238 --> 00:27:45.810
Also, sometimes I want
to be social on Sundays.

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:48.930
I want to head out on a hike
with my partner or I want

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:50.820
to meet up with friends
and hike with them.

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:53.010
And so taking a long hike
on Sunday is something

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:54.360
that also could be quite social.

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:56.250
And then I don't have to
worry about also getting

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:58.770
in my workout when I'm
heading out on a hike

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.391
with my partner or going
out to meet with friends

00:28:01.391 --> 00:28:03.033
or things of that sort.

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:07.470
I will say that there's a specific tool

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:10.453
or a specific change that
you can make to this Sunday

00:28:10.453 --> 00:28:13.620
long endurance, or at least
what I consider long for me,

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:14.498
I mean it's by no means a marathon

00:28:14.498 --> 00:28:18.510
or an Ironman, but this
long endurance training,

00:28:18.510 --> 00:28:20.640
and that's the use of a weight vest.

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:23.040
So something that I've
really started utilizing

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.290
more recently, and by more recently,

00:28:25.290 --> 00:28:27.450
I really mean within the last year or so,

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:29.736
is I purchased one of
these weight vests that can

00:28:29.736 --> 00:28:33.060
be anywhere from 10 to 50 pounds.

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:35.430
What I use in the weight
vest is irrelevant.

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:39.330
But it certainly changes
the level of effort required

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:41.010
when taking a hike or even a walk.

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:42.810
Now there's an additional
benefit of the weight vest,

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:45.660
which is that if you
are going out for a hike

00:28:45.660 --> 00:28:47.490
or even for a walk for social reasons

00:28:47.490 --> 00:28:48.690
and you're with somebody that's not quite

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:50.700
at the same fitness level that you are,

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:53.152
frankly it's a little bit
rude to just keep walking

00:28:53.152 --> 00:28:55.290
ahead of them and running
back or running ahead

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:56.310
and running back.

00:28:56.310 --> 00:28:58.260
Oftentimes you really want
to spend time with the person

00:28:58.260 --> 00:28:59.280
and you don't want them to feel

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:00.570
as if they're holding you up.

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:03.180
And so the weight vest
is a terrific way to get

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:04.860
some additional work, then,

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:07.140
as you'll find if you wear a weight vest,

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:10.500
it is additional work
on, say, a shorter hike.

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:12.900
So maybe the person
you're with only has time

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.040
for an hour long hike,

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:17.010
or maybe they just don't have
the fitness to do a two hour,

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:17.940
three hour hike.

00:29:17.940 --> 00:29:19.920
So I'll throw on the
weight vest and I'll head

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:21.990
out for a walk with them
or a hike with them,

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.670
or sometimes I'll go out on a long hike

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:25.380
with a weight vest myself.

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.870
So again, the point of this, for me,

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:30.720
Sunday, although it could fall
on any day for you, workout,

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:32.997
is really to build up
that long form endurance.

00:29:32.997 --> 00:29:33.960
And this fits well

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:37.500
with what Dr. Andy Galpin
and Dr. Peter Attia referred

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.800
to as the real need to
get in some long endurance

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:43.882
type work at some point
or even multiple points

00:29:43.882 --> 00:29:45.030
throughout the week.

00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:48.482
For me, this long Sunday
jog of 60 to 75 minutes

00:29:48.482 --> 00:29:52.890
or long Sunday hike or
weighted walk or weighted hike

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:54.690
really accomplishes that goal.

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:57.183
Sometimes leads to a
little bit of soreness,

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:00.600
particularly in my
calves or if I'm wearing

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:03.840
the weight vest, sometimes
my midsection will get

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:05.820
sore because I'm trying to remain upright.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:09.000
So I think it also builds
up some muscular endurance,

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:11.280
not just cardiovascular
endurance, but again,

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:14.340
throughout the entire time
that I'm jogging or hiking,

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.980
what I'm trying to get to
is a place where I can feel

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:19.620
that my pulse rate is definitely elevated,

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:21.810
but it's not so elevated
that I have to stop

00:30:21.810 --> 00:30:23.160
because I'm out of breath.

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.230
And because I know some
people out there might be

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:27.090
really neurotic about this sort of thing,

00:30:27.090 --> 00:30:29.100
if you have to stop because
you're out of breath,

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:30.747
that doesn't mean that
you blew the workout,

00:30:30.747 --> 00:30:31.980
that you now aren't getting endurance.

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:34.350
Of course, you're
getting benefits from it.

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:36.480
So I'm not absolutely
neurotic about always

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:38.730
staying exactly in that heart rate zone.

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:40.920
I might stop and have a
conversation for a moment

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:42.090
if it's a longer hike,

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:43.800
although I really try
and keep moving and I try

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:45.562
and push myself just a little bit further

00:30:45.562 --> 00:30:48.690
than where I'm exceedingly comfortable.

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:49.523
And so for me,

00:30:49.523 --> 00:30:53.366
doing this long Sunday
hike or jog really provides

00:30:53.366 --> 00:30:56.490
a foundation, a base
for endurance that then

00:30:56.490 --> 00:30:58.950
the other endurance workouts
that I'll describe later,

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:01.770
that take place later in
the week, can build on.

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:02.940
Now, as I mentioned earlier,

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:05.160
we will get back to the
mechanisms that this taps

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:07.620
into and why this is so useful.

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:09.660
There are multiple benefits
to doing these kinds

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:13.080
of endurance type workouts
in zone two cardio.

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:16.050
But by putting it at the
start of my week, again,

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:19.303
my week starts on Sunday,
I'm sure that regardless

00:31:19.303 --> 00:31:21.412
of how the rest of the week goes,

00:31:21.412 --> 00:31:24.030
that I got my endurance training in,

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:26.250
and of course I'm going to want to,

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:28.050
and I will do endurance
training other days

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:30.180
during the week, but if
something comes up or I happen

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:33.240
to get sick or I'm really
behind in terms of work

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:36.460
and I can't get other workouts
in, this Sunday long jog

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:39.495
or hike really provides that fundamental,

00:31:39.495 --> 00:31:42.000
I can honestly say foundation,

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:45.150
for cardiovascular fitness
and endurance that I can

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:47.850
hang my hat on and say, okay,
I've got that one in the bag,

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:50.580
and I can then look to other
days of the week to focus

00:31:50.580 --> 00:31:52.170
on other aspects of fitness.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:53.850
Now, a really important point to make

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:57.210
about this Sunday endurance
workout is that allows

00:31:57.210 --> 00:32:02.210
you to check off a box and
that box is 75 or so minutes

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:06.330
of zone two cardio, because
as you may have heard,

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:08.520
either in this podcast or
from others out there like

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:11.516
Dr. Peter Attia, getting
180 to 200 minutes

00:32:11.516 --> 00:32:16.230
of zone two cardio per week
has enormous positive effects

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:18.340
on longevity and enormous positive effects

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:21.270
on general health, again,

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:22.800
in terms of cardiovascular function,

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.710
but also metabolic fuel utilization,

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:28.350
also in terms of your
musculature and your ability

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:30.960
to use your body over
long distances for long

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:32.010
periods of time.

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:36.120
So while it doesn't complete
all 180 to 200 minutes

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:39.150
per week, it certainly
gets you a good distance,

00:32:39.150 --> 00:32:41.250
pun intended, toward that goal.

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:43.140
Now, I want to acknowledge
that some people might

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:44.730
be starting a fitness program,

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:47.970
and so 60 to 75 minutes of
jogging might be too long

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:50.460
or a three hour weight vested hike,

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.440
or some people might even
do what's called a ruck,

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:55.260
like you wear a rucksack,
that might be too much,

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:59.430
in which case certainly
start with less and go

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:01.440
on flat ground and go
at the rate that allows

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:02.605
you to get into zone two,

00:33:02.605 --> 00:33:04.830
but that is not excessively
difficult for you.

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:06.000
And then as you build up fitness,

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:08.430
you can add time or you can add weight

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:09.330
through a weight vest,

00:33:09.330 --> 00:33:12.120
or if you don't want to
buy a weight vest or can't

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:14.353
afford one, there's a
simple solution to that,

00:33:14.353 --> 00:33:16.530
I actually have a good
anecdote about that.

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:19.170
One time I was heading out
for a hike with a friend

00:33:19.170 --> 00:33:21.540
of mine, he was a former
SEAL team operator,

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:23.167
I'll never forget this,
and he said, oh yeah,

00:33:23.167 --> 00:33:25.590
"I'll bring you a sack."

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:26.970
And I thought he meant like a sack lunch,

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:27.810
like he was going to bring lunch.

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:29.520
And I showed up and he
basically gave me a backpack

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:30.930
that was loaded with a bunch of stuff

00:33:30.930 --> 00:33:32.580
and the backpack weighed about 40 pounds.

00:33:32.580 --> 00:33:33.600
And then we took a hike.

00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:36.960
So I was thinking lunch, he
was thinking weighted backpack,

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:39.870
and weighted backpack or
even just any kind of strong

00:33:39.870 --> 00:33:42.030
sack that you can put over
your shoulders or even carry

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:45.000
in your arms, it's going to
work exceedingly well to build

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:46.740
in some extra requirement for effort.

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:49.295
So you certainly don't have
to purchase a weight vest

00:33:49.295 --> 00:33:52.870
in order to get the benefits
of bringing additional

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:57.060
weight along with you on these
long cardiovascular events.

00:33:57.060 --> 00:33:59.820
But again, build up over
time, you can add time,

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:00.870
you can add weight,

00:34:00.870 --> 00:34:02.880
and that's also a really nice
feature of adding weight,

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:05.989
which is at some point your
schedule might be such,

00:34:05.989 --> 00:34:10.350
or you just don't really
want to keep adding more

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:14.460
and more and more time on
this long endurance Sunday,

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:18.420
in this case, workout,
in that case add weight.

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:19.860
You can also, as you build up fitness,

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:20.910
you can add speed to it,

00:34:20.910 --> 00:34:24.030
your zone two and what
zone two is won't shift,

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:26.850
but what work is required
from you in order to get

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:28.440
into zone two will shift.

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.030
That is as you get more and more fit,

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.370
you'll have to move faster
and or bring more weight

00:34:32.370 --> 00:34:35.190
in order to stay in zone two
and that will simply tell

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:37.800
you that you are indeed
improving your endurance.

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:40.620
Okay, so then Monday
rolls around and I, like

00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:41.910
most everyone else out there,

00:34:41.910 --> 00:34:45.810
I work on Monday, I get
right into my emails

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:47.550
and preparation for podcasts and running

00:34:47.550 --> 00:34:49.050
my laboratory, et cetera.

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:52.050
However, I make sure that at some point

00:34:52.050 --> 00:34:55.507
on Monday, and for me that
some point is typically

00:34:55.507 --> 00:34:59.228
and ideally early in the
morning, so 7:00 AM or so,

00:34:59.228 --> 00:35:02.640
I train my legs on Monday,
so that includes quadriceps,

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:04.260
hamstrings, and calves.

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:05.910
Why do I do that workout on Monday?

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:07.410
And what is that workout designed to do?

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:10.590
Well, that workout is
really designed to make sure

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:13.650
that I'm either maintaining
or building strength

00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:15.180
in my legs.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:18.150
And this is not simply
for aesthetic reasons.

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:20.700
This is not simply to
grow bigger calves or grow

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:22.350
bigger quadriceps and hamstrings,

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:24.720
although it can accomplish
that as well depending

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:25.620
on how you train.

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:27.120
We'll talk about details of training.

00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:29.220
The reason for training legs
on Monday is several fold.

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:32.070
First of all, they are
the largest muscle groups

00:35:32.070 --> 00:35:34.290
of the body, and by training your legs

00:35:34.290 --> 00:35:37.110
on Monday, it sets in
motion a large number

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:40.620
of metabolic processes that
carry you some distance

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:41.880
even through the whole week in terms

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:45.420
of elevating metabolism,
in terms of amplifying

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:48.660
certain hormonal events
in your body, et cetera,

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:50.910
that are really beneficial.

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:51.810
In addition to that,

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:54.240
I'm of the belief that the
legs are the foundation

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:57.751
of the body, and provided
you can train legs safely,

00:35:57.751 --> 00:36:00.150
that training legs is vitally important,

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:02.490
not just for strength of the legs,

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:04.740
but also for strength of your entire body.

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:06.720
Again, some of that is
through systemic hormonal

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:08.820
effects because if you're
going to train the large

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:11.280
muscle groups of your body
under substantial loads,

00:36:11.280 --> 00:36:13.290
you will get systemic release of hormones,

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:15.750
not just testosterone, although
certainly testosterone,

00:36:15.750 --> 00:36:17.528
but also things like growth hormone...

00:36:17.528 --> 00:36:19.804
You get increases in
all sorts of so-called

00:36:19.804 --> 00:36:22.380
anabolic hormones that
even if you're somebody

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:24.240
who's not trying to increase muscle size,

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:25.860
because I realize a lot
of people are not trying

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:28.470
to do that, these are hormones that shift

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:31.320
your metabolism and your
overall tendon strength

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:33.308
and ligament strength
and overall musculature

00:36:33.308 --> 00:36:36.960
into what I would call
a strong foundation.

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:38.850
So for me, Monday is leg workout.

00:36:38.850 --> 00:36:41.340
It also just feels good
to get the leg workout

00:36:41.340 --> 00:36:43.260
out of the way earlier in the week,

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:44.970
and it accomplishes another goal,

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:48.870
which is that I sometimes
will take one or two days off

00:36:48.870 --> 00:36:50.910
of a leg workout because
they can be very intense

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:52.470
and they are large muscle groups,

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:54.240
and I'll explain what
I do on the off days,

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:55.470
they're not pure off days,

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:57.870
they actually include some
recovery type training

00:36:57.870 --> 00:37:00.000
or even some all out training.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:04.350
But by training legs on
Monday, I'm able to get

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:06.870
what I consider the hardest strength

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:10.020
and hypertrophy workout
out of the way, and, again,

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:12.480
set all those positive
physiological effects in motion

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:13.860
for the entire week.

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:17.160
The other thing is that no
workout exists in isolation.

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:19.620
What you do one day is
going to be determined

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:21.000
by what you did the previous day.

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:22.680
And even though the previous
day I may have taken

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:24.690
a three hour weight vested hike,

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:27.207
never are my legs so
sore from that long slow

00:37:27.207 --> 00:37:30.450
endurance work, because
it is long and slow,

00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:32.370
that I'm unable to train legs.

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:34.320
Contrast that with a, say,

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:36.840
high intensity interval training workout,

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:40.110
which comes later in the week,
and my legs might be sore.

00:37:40.110 --> 00:37:41.970
In fact, they might not
even be recovered such

00:37:41.970 --> 00:37:44.340
that I'm able to do a real legwork,

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:45.510
I want to say a real workout.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:47.340
I'll describe what that means in a moment.

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:48.873
So legs come on Monday, and I think

00:37:48.873 --> 00:37:51.870
that for those of you that
are using or interested

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:54.030
in using resistance training,

00:37:54.030 --> 00:37:57.030
I suggest getting your leg
workout done early in the week.

00:37:57.030 --> 00:37:59.480
And for those of you that
have heard the phrase,

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:02.940
don't skip leg day, I will
go a step further and say,

00:38:02.940 --> 00:38:04.530
don't skip leg day, in fact,

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:06.510
make leg day your first day of strength

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:07.740
and hypertrophy training.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:08.640
Put it on Monday.

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:10.530
Okay, so now that we're talking
about resistance training,

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:13.080
the question is going to
come up about sets and reps

00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:14.146
and all of that business.

00:38:14.146 --> 00:38:17.130
That was covered in a lot
of detail on the podcast

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:18.959
with Dr. Andy Galpin, and
I'm going to get into some

00:38:18.959 --> 00:38:20.760
of that detail now,

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:25.760
but I'm going to wait until
I describe the entire set

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:28.757
of workouts for the week before
I go into even more detail,

00:38:28.757 --> 00:38:31.712
because there's a way of
what's called periodizing

00:38:31.712 --> 00:38:34.511
that is changing the
sets and reps, et cetera,

00:38:34.511 --> 00:38:38.340
across the week, and
indeed from month to month,

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:39.690
that's really optimal.

00:38:39.690 --> 00:38:42.180
But I don't want to make it seem as if all

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:44.490
of that just pertains to the leg workout.

00:38:44.490 --> 00:38:47.220
It actually pertains to all
of the resistance training.

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:49.428
So I'll just give you a couple of teasers

00:38:49.428 --> 00:38:52.210
about the key principles
of resistance training

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:56.880
that I think are almost
universally, if not universally,

00:38:56.880 --> 00:38:59.790
then generally accepted
in the strength training

00:38:59.790 --> 00:39:01.440
and physiology community.

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:03.840
And then later I'll get back to some

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:07.068
of the overarching
principles that apply to all

00:39:07.068 --> 00:39:10.830
strength and hypertrophy
workouts across the week,

00:39:10.830 --> 00:39:13.260
including the ones for the
torso, the arms, et cetera.

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:16.200
Okay, so legs fall on Monday, I should say

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:19.890
that leg workouts, like all
resistance training workouts,

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:23.190
for me, consist of about, again,

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:24.570
I'm not neurotically attached to this,

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:27.980
but about 10 minutes of
warming up and then about 50,

00:39:27.980 --> 00:39:30.990
five zero, to 60 minutes of real work.

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:33.360
Now, of course, some of that is
going to be rest between sets,

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:35.940
but by real work, I mean really hard work,

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:37.410
not necessarily to failure,

00:39:37.410 --> 00:39:38.880
we'll talk about failure in a little bit,

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:42.053
but hard work where I'm
struggling to complete

00:39:42.053 --> 00:39:45.150
the final repetitions,
if not going to failure

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:48.030
to continue to move
the weight repetitions.

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:51.210
And again, the entire work
portion of that workout

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:53.220
is about 50 to 60 minutes.

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:54.053
Why?

00:39:54.053 --> 00:39:55.170
Well, past 60 minutes,

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:56.820
you start getting increases in cortisol

00:39:56.820 --> 00:39:58.050
that really impede recovery.

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:02.100
And I personally am somebody
that does not recover

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:04.620
very well from high intensity exercise.

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:09.240
I realize that within the
literature, it is believed,

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:10.920
and I think generally accepted,

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:14.010
that when you stimulate muscle hypertrophy

00:40:14.010 --> 00:40:18.055
or strength increases, it
impacts the nervous system,

00:40:18.055 --> 00:40:20.330
it also causes things like
protein synthesis, et cetera.

00:40:20.330 --> 00:40:22.980
There are a number of
different forms of adaptation

00:40:22.980 --> 00:40:26.073
that occur to give you muscle
strength and size changes.

00:40:27.150 --> 00:40:29.610
And these days people
talk a lot about needing

00:40:29.610 --> 00:40:32.070
to stimulate muscle
growth or muscle strength

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:33.930
at least every 48 hours.

00:40:33.930 --> 00:40:36.600
But I can tell you that I
recover route there slowly,

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:39.660
and I benefit from working
the same muscle group

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:42.930
about twice per week, with longer,

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:45.630
or I should say more days of rest

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:47.130
in between those workouts.

00:40:47.130 --> 00:40:49.890
So if I train legs on
Monday, believe it or not,

00:40:49.890 --> 00:40:52.080
I'm only training legs on Monday.

00:40:52.080 --> 00:40:55.080
I do not have a second leg
workout during the week.

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:59.100
However, on Friday, I do a high intensity

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:02.400
interval training session
that serves two purposes.

00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:05.370
One is it serves the purpose
of triggering a certain

00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:07.881
type of endurance and
getting my heart rate

00:41:07.881 --> 00:41:09.540
very, very high.

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:10.680
And in addition to that,

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:12.840
because of the way I do that workout,

00:41:12.840 --> 00:41:15.570
it acts as a sort of supplement or a more

00:41:15.570 --> 00:41:20.400
moderate intensity workout
for quadriceps, hamstrings,

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:23.970
and calves, such that I at
least never lose strength,

00:41:23.970 --> 00:41:26.430
and in fact, generally
build strength from one leg

00:41:26.430 --> 00:41:29.760
workout to the next, provided
I'm doing things correctly.

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:33.720
So what I'm not referring
to is the kind of classic

00:41:33.720 --> 00:41:36.253
super high intensity training
once per week and then

00:41:36.253 --> 00:41:38.790
not actually training
that muscle group again.

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:41.130
For me, it's really training
each muscle group twice

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:44.940
per week, once directly
and then once indirectly,

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:47.040
either during another
weight training workout

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:49.170
or during a cardiovascular,

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:50.610
I should say endurance training workout.

00:41:50.610 --> 00:41:54.000
So again, legs on Monday, the
workout is 50 to 60 minutes,

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:55.830
after a brief warmup.

00:41:55.830 --> 00:41:58.950
I generally pick two
exercises per muscle group.

00:41:58.950 --> 00:42:01.890
So again, I'm doing calves,
I'm doing quadriceps,

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:03.330
and I'm doing hamstrings.

00:42:03.330 --> 00:42:06.360
You should pick the
exercises that work for you.

00:42:06.360 --> 00:42:07.920
So that's why I'm actually
not going to share

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:09.060
which exercises I use.

00:42:09.060 --> 00:42:11.700
I'll give you a couple suggestions
about the ones I do use,

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:13.380
but really, exercise selection,

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:14.780
as Dr. Andy Galpin pointed out,

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:17.052
is a very important variable.

00:42:17.052 --> 00:42:19.620
And the key thing to
emphasize for that variable

00:42:19.620 --> 00:42:22.260
is that you need to be able to
perform the movement safely.

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:24.210
So I know there's a huge debate out there,

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:26.160
and people love to argue
about whether or not one

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:28.800
can squat or deadlift
for long periods of time,

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:29.940
or should or should not.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:31.590
Some people say you absolutely should.

00:42:31.590 --> 00:42:34.890
I personally do not squat
and do not deadlift.

00:42:34.890 --> 00:42:37.530
I've actually never done much
squatting or deadlifting,

00:42:37.530 --> 00:42:39.540
and I know some people
out there are probably

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:42.440
rolling their eyes or switching
the channel at this point.

00:42:43.290 --> 00:42:45.870
But I can say that for me,

00:42:45.870 --> 00:42:48.420
I've been able to achieve the strength

00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:50.973
and hypertrophy goals
that I've been seeking,

00:42:50.973 --> 00:42:54.690
doing things like leg
extensions and hack squats

00:42:54.690 --> 00:42:56.610
or for hamstrings, doing
things like leg curls

00:42:56.610 --> 00:42:58.890
and glute-ham raises or
for calves doing standing

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:00.990
and seated calf raises and so on.

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:03.180
I think a key principle
that everyone should pay

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:05.400
attention to is one that was taught to me

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:08.880
by an excellent strength coach years ago,

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:11.430
and I still use this and
at least it works for me.

00:43:11.430 --> 00:43:12.810
For each muscle group,

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:16.200
try and find an exercise in
which you get that muscle

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:18.510
into a weighted stretch position.

00:43:18.510 --> 00:43:20.970
So this would be, for instance,
the standing calf raise,

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:21.803
down at the bottom,

00:43:21.803 --> 00:43:24.360
it's weighted and you're
in a deep stretch provided

00:43:24.360 --> 00:43:26.640
you're doing the movement correctly.

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:28.673
As well as another exercise

00:43:28.673 --> 00:43:31.560
where you're getting
contraction in the shortened

00:43:31.560 --> 00:43:32.580
position of the muscle.

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:34.680
So for the hamstrings,
that would be the leg curl,

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:37.500
for the calves, it would
be a seated calf raise,

00:43:37.500 --> 00:43:42.030
for the quadriceps, the leg extension is,

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:43.980
if the machine is designed
right and you're doing

00:43:43.980 --> 00:43:45.902
it correctly, the peak
contraction is largely going

00:43:45.902 --> 00:43:49.140
to occur at the legs extended position,

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.529
but then another exercise
for each muscle group

00:43:51.529 --> 00:43:54.201
that puts the muscle
into more of a stretched

00:43:54.201 --> 00:43:58.752
or at least a larger range
of motion or compound

00:43:58.752 --> 00:43:59.700
type movement,

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:01.650
but ideally where there's
some stretch there.

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:04.350
So I guess I will tell
you what exercise I do,

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:06.180
for the quadriceps is
going to be leg extensions

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:07.013
and hack squats.

00:44:07.013 --> 00:44:09.360
I use hack squats because
I don't do free bar squats

00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:11.850
for safety reasons, and I
like the hack squat machine.

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:15.870
I'll do leg curls and glute-ham
raises for hamstrings,

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:17.940
and I'll do standing calf
raises and seated calf

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:18.773
raises for the calves.

00:44:18.773 --> 00:44:20.550
Again, those are the movements that I use

00:44:20.550 --> 00:44:23.310
because I can perform them
safely in the repetition

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:25.770
ranges and with the weights
that are required for me

00:44:25.770 --> 00:44:28.860
to either maintain or build
leg strength and calf strength.

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:31.590
But you might decide that
for you, dead lifts are

00:44:31.590 --> 00:44:33.330
absolutely essential and terrific,

00:44:33.330 --> 00:44:36.510
or squats free bar squats
are absolutely terrific

00:44:36.510 --> 00:44:37.950
or front squats.

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:41.370
I'm not here to tell you which
exercises to do or not do.

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:44.190
I am telling you that it's probably wise

00:44:44.190 --> 00:44:48.810
to at least consider doing
at least two exercises

00:44:48.810 --> 00:44:51.570
per muscle group, probably three maximum,

00:44:51.570 --> 00:44:53.490
if you ask me if you're
doing your entire legs

00:44:53.490 --> 00:44:54.600
and calves in one day.

00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:56.340
But to think about doing one exercise

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:58.050
where the muscle's brought
into that shortened

00:44:58.050 --> 00:44:59.640
peak contraction position,

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:01.830
like leg curls or leg
extensions or seated calf raise,

00:45:01.830 --> 00:45:04.050
and then another exercise
for each muscle group

00:45:04.050 --> 00:45:06.090
where there's more of
a elongation and maybe

00:45:06.090 --> 00:45:08.010
even a stretch on the muscle group.

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:09.930
In fact, that's a principle
that you'll hear me talk

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:12.600
about later when I talk
about training other muscle

00:45:12.600 --> 00:45:14.430
groups for strength and hypertrophy.

00:45:14.430 --> 00:45:17.370
So now you know approximately
how long to train,

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:19.290
you might be somebody who
can get away with training

00:45:19.290 --> 00:45:20.850
for an hour and a half,

00:45:20.850 --> 00:45:22.650
and that won't impede your recovery.

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:24.555
For me, that really starts
to impede my recovery.

00:45:24.555 --> 00:45:26.861
Also, if I'm staying on task,

00:45:26.861 --> 00:45:29.970
that 60 minute limit
really works well for me.

00:45:29.970 --> 00:45:32.940
Do I occasionally train for 75 minutes?

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:35.070
Yes, because if I'm waiting
for a piece of equipment,

00:45:35.070 --> 00:45:37.080
sometimes I have to just wait longer.

00:45:37.080 --> 00:45:37.913
So that happens.

00:45:37.913 --> 00:45:39.870
But I really try and
keep the total duration

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:41.640
of the workout shorter.

00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:43.470
How many sets and reps and rest intervals?

00:45:43.470 --> 00:45:45.840
Well, that was covered by
Dr. Andy Galpin as well.

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:47.790
Without getting into the total science,

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:52.440
here's a brief summary
of how to structure that.

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:55.950
It's pretty clear that
if you're going to do

00:45:55.950 --> 00:46:00.300
lower repetitions and heavier weight,

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:03.450
that you're going to want
to do a bit more volume.

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:05.610
I know that this spits
in the face of what a lot

00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:08.310
of people think, but so if
you're going to do five sets

00:46:08.310 --> 00:46:10.029
of five, and I would
consider five repetitions

00:46:10.029 --> 00:46:14.130
low repetition range, heavier weight,

00:46:14.130 --> 00:46:16.410
and if you're going to train
with higher repetitions,

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:17.610
you can do fewer sets.

00:46:17.610 --> 00:46:19.713
That certainly works for me.

00:46:20.730 --> 00:46:23.850
I generally follow a program
where for about a month,

00:46:23.850 --> 00:46:25.380
so three to four weeks,

00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:27.510
I will do all my resistance training

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:31.710
in the repetition range of
about four to eight repetitions.

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:34.830
So that's rather heavy. A few more sets.

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:37.710
So it might be anywhere from three to four

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:39.840
sets per exercise.

00:46:39.840 --> 00:46:41.430
Again, still just two exercises.

00:46:41.430 --> 00:46:43.410
And longer rest between sets,

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:46.620
anywhere from two minutes
to maybe even four minutes

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:48.720
if it's really heavy leg work.

00:46:48.720 --> 00:46:50.460
And then for the next month,

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:55.050
switch to repetition range
that's closer to 8 to 12,

00:46:55.050 --> 00:46:57.690
maybe even 15 repetitions per set,

00:46:57.690 --> 00:46:59.910
but do fewer sets overall,

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:01.980
so maybe just two to
three sets per exercise.

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:05.280
Again, just two exercises
per muscle group typically.

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:09.420
And shorten the rest between
sets so that it's more

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:10.590
in the 90 second,

00:47:10.590 --> 00:47:13.290
maybe even as short as 60
second rest between sets,

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:16.170
but typically 90 seconds
to about two minutes or two

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:17.003
and a half minutes.

00:47:17.003 --> 00:47:18.734
So basically it's one month heavier,

00:47:18.734 --> 00:47:21.060
the next month, slightly lighter,

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:22.260
although I wouldn't say lighter,

00:47:22.260 --> 00:47:26.100
I would say moderate weight
and moderate rep range.

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:28.110
That tends to work well for me.

00:47:28.110 --> 00:47:31.050
It also adheres to a
principal that came up

00:47:31.050 --> 00:47:32.310
during the discussion, again,

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:34.590
with Dr. Andy Galpin,
that for hypertrophy,

00:47:34.590 --> 00:47:36.510
you really can use
repetition ranges anywhere

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:38.913
from 5 to 30, three zero, reps.

00:47:39.810 --> 00:47:42.930
But he emphasized changing
the repetition ranges

00:47:42.930 --> 00:47:44.850
in order to offset boredom.

00:47:44.850 --> 00:47:46.530
Frankly, I like to train heavier.

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:48.570
I enjoy training in the
four to eight rep range.

00:47:48.570 --> 00:47:50.760
However, I notice that
if I do that for more

00:47:50.760 --> 00:47:52.620
than four weeks in a
row and I don't switch

00:47:52.620 --> 00:47:55.620
over to training in the
8 to 12, maybe in 15

00:47:55.620 --> 00:47:57.390
repetition range for about a month,

00:47:57.390 --> 00:47:59.280
well then I can't make
continuous progress.

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:00.960
I start to actually lose ground.

00:48:00.960 --> 00:48:03.150
But by switching back and forth,

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:06.120
I actually can make
continuous progress at least

00:48:06.120 --> 00:48:07.560
across the year.

00:48:07.560 --> 00:48:09.480
So I hope that that principle,

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:12.693
or I should say that protocol
was communicated clearly.

00:48:13.565 --> 00:48:15.180
It works very well, I assure you.

00:48:15.180 --> 00:48:17.910
Does that mean that I
never get 10 repetitions

00:48:17.910 --> 00:48:20.490
on a week when I'm supposed
to train in the four

00:48:20.490 --> 00:48:21.540
to eight repetition range?

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:22.574
No.

00:48:22.574 --> 00:48:24.930
Occasionally I'll venture up
into the 10 repetition range,

00:48:24.930 --> 00:48:28.230
but I really try and cluster
the low repetition work

00:48:28.230 --> 00:48:29.550
for about a month, again,

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:31.530
across all workouts and all exercises

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:33.120
and the slightly higher,

00:48:33.120 --> 00:48:35.490
I would even say moderate
repetition work across

00:48:35.490 --> 00:48:36.630
to the next month.

00:48:36.630 --> 00:48:38.520
One thing that you'll
notice since we are talking

00:48:38.520 --> 00:48:40.200
about total fitness programming

00:48:40.200 --> 00:48:43.950
is that during the months
where you are doing

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:45.540
moderate repetitions, you'll notice

00:48:45.540 --> 00:48:49.980
that your endurance work
will actually be facilitated.

00:48:49.980 --> 00:48:52.140
And I do not think that's a coincidence.

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:53.490
In fact, it's not a coincidence.

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:55.543
It's because when you
are training very heavy

00:48:55.543 --> 00:48:59.220
or in the heavier range,
lower repetitions, et cetera,

00:48:59.220 --> 00:49:01.560
you're tapping into different
processes in those muscles.

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:03.420
So when you head out for
that long Sunday hike,

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:05.220
or as you'll soon hear,

00:49:05.220 --> 00:49:07.170
whereas on Friday you're
going to do high intensity

00:49:07.170 --> 00:49:09.090
interval training, what you'll notice

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:11.490
is during certain months
of weight training,

00:49:11.490 --> 00:49:15.240
when you're training more
heavy, those workouts will feel,

00:49:15.240 --> 00:49:17.700
literally will feel
different than they will

00:49:17.700 --> 00:49:18.660
during the months when you're doing

00:49:18.660 --> 00:49:20.403
moderate repetition work.

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:23.220
I am not a competitive athlete.

00:49:23.220 --> 00:49:25.470
I'm not running races
or triathlons like some

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:26.460
of my friends.

00:49:26.460 --> 00:49:27.690
I'm very impressed by them.

00:49:27.690 --> 00:49:29.610
I'm really just trying to get overall

00:49:29.610 --> 00:49:32.070
cardiovascular fitness, overall strength,

00:49:32.070 --> 00:49:35.409
overall hypertrophy where I
need it, maintain muscle size,

00:49:35.409 --> 00:49:36.510
et cetera,

00:49:36.510 --> 00:49:38.370
in muscle groups where I'm
just trying to maintain.

00:49:38.370 --> 00:49:39.840
That's really my goal.

00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:42.660
So I'm not trying to optimize
any of these workouts

00:49:42.660 --> 00:49:45.600
for any one performance
feature, but in a little bit,

00:49:45.600 --> 00:49:50.160
we'll talk about how you
can change various aspects,

00:49:50.160 --> 00:49:52.260
that is, variables of these
protocols, in order to say,

00:49:52.260 --> 00:49:54.570
for instance, really emphasize hypertrophy

00:49:54.570 --> 00:49:56.700
or really emphasize endurance.

00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:58.560
Okay, so with what I would call a standard

00:49:58.560 --> 00:50:00.570
endurance workout done
on Sunday, and I say

00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:02.520
standard because most people,
when they hear endurance,

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:04.260
they think of the ability to endure,

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:07.920
to continue in a repeated
movement or exercise

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:09.120
over some period of time,

00:50:09.120 --> 00:50:11.280
with that workout done on Sunday, and then

00:50:11.280 --> 00:50:14.220
with the leg workout done
on Monday, you can feel

00:50:14.220 --> 00:50:16.410
really good about how you're
heading into the week.

00:50:16.410 --> 00:50:20.279
However, after training
legs on Monday, I experience

00:50:20.279 --> 00:50:23.737
that doing cardiovascular
workouts the next day

00:50:23.737 --> 00:50:28.500
is either inefficient or at
least doesn't really allow

00:50:28.500 --> 00:50:30.720
me to completely recover
from my leg workout.

00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:31.950
Now, I realize that
some people are going to

00:50:31.950 --> 00:50:34.800
immediately scoff at that, and in fact,

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:37.260
there are really beautiful
papers out there talking

00:50:37.260 --> 00:50:40.410
about how one can
actually do a fair amount

00:50:40.410 --> 00:50:42.718
of cardiovascular exercise
without interfering

00:50:42.718 --> 00:50:45.960
with their strength and
speed and hypertrophy

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:49.200
improvements and vice versa.

00:50:49.200 --> 00:50:51.281
In fact, there's a terrific
review that was mentioned

00:50:51.281 --> 00:50:53.700
on the podcast with Dr. Andy Galpin.

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:56.847
This is a review that we'll
provide a citation to,

00:50:56.847 --> 00:50:58.830
and a reference and a link to,

00:50:58.830 --> 00:51:02.010
which is the review by Murach
and Bagley, which talks

00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:03.690
about whether or not there's interference

00:51:03.690 --> 00:51:05.520
between strength and endurance workouts.

00:51:05.520 --> 00:51:08.490
Really interesting review
if you want to peruse that.

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:09.900
But with all that said,

00:51:09.900 --> 00:51:12.720
I like to take Tuesday as a no endurance,

00:51:12.720 --> 00:51:14.253
no resistance training day,

00:51:15.210 --> 00:51:17.640
but that doesn't mean that
I'm not doing anything

00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:20.280
for my overall health and fitness.

00:51:20.280 --> 00:51:25.280
On Tuesdays, I do a series
of heat cold contrast.

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:27.420
In other words, I get really,

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:29.460
really warm and then I
get really, really cold,

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:30.810
I get really, really
warm and I get really,

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:32.430
really cold repeatedly.

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:36.300
And the way I do that is by
getting into a hot sauna.

00:51:36.300 --> 00:51:37.740
So for me, that's really hot,

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:39.300
but I've built up my heat conditioning,

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:41.040
so please don't do this
unless you've built up

00:51:41.040 --> 00:51:43.380
your ability to withstand heat.

00:51:43.380 --> 00:51:45.840
And I'll get in for about 20 minutes.

00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:47.970
Sometimes 15, but usually 20 minutes.

00:51:47.970 --> 00:51:50.760
Then I get out and then I
will get into an ice bath

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:52.620
or a cold water bath

00:51:52.620 --> 00:51:56.370
that's about 45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:51:56.370 --> 00:51:59.340
Again, don't get into water that's so cold

00:51:59.340 --> 00:52:00.630
that you go into shock.

00:52:00.630 --> 00:52:04.650
I'll explain what a good
cold stimulus could be

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:05.982
for you and how to determine that.

00:52:05.982 --> 00:52:10.083
Or if I don't have access
to my sauna and my ice bath,

00:52:10.980 --> 00:52:13.860
what I can do if I'm
traveling is I will take a hot

00:52:13.860 --> 00:52:15.870
bath and then alternate with cold shower,

00:52:15.870 --> 00:52:17.610
hot bath, cold shower.

00:52:17.610 --> 00:52:19.710
It's hard to do hot bath ice bath

00:52:19.710 --> 00:52:20.670
unless you have two baths.

00:52:20.670 --> 00:52:21.810
I don't know any hotel rooms,

00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:23.430
at least I've never stayed
in one that has two baths,

00:52:23.430 --> 00:52:25.080
although I'm sure they're out there.

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:28.466
But for me, this is heat cold contrast.

00:52:28.466 --> 00:52:31.590
And really what this day
is about is two things.

00:52:31.590 --> 00:52:34.050
First of all, I'm trying
to accelerate recovery

00:52:34.050 --> 00:52:36.180
from the leg workout I did previously.

00:52:36.180 --> 00:52:38.534
Also, if you listen to our episode

00:52:38.534 --> 00:52:41.160
of the Huberman Lab podcast
about deliberate heat

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:42.870
exposure or you listen to our episode

00:52:42.870 --> 00:52:46.620
of the Huberman Lab podcast
about deliberate cold exposure,

00:52:46.620 --> 00:52:49.620
I talk about some of the
benefits of heat and cold,

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:51.480
and I get into a lot of details

00:52:51.480 --> 00:52:52.617
about how you can access heat.

00:52:52.617 --> 00:52:54.600
You can do baths, you can do saunas,

00:52:54.600 --> 00:52:56.160
you can even take hot showers.

00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:57.510
If you don't have access to any of that,

00:52:57.510 --> 00:52:59.910
you could even wrap your
body from the neck down

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:02.610
in a garbage bag, plastic
garbage bags, believe it or not,

00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:03.510
wrestlers used to do this,

00:53:03.510 --> 00:53:05.040
put on some sweats and go running.

00:53:05.040 --> 00:53:06.000
That'll get you warm.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:08.623
Again, be careful not to
overheat and then you can

00:53:08.623 --> 00:53:10.140
get into a cold shower.

00:53:10.140 --> 00:53:11.190
So there's a lot of ways,

00:53:11.190 --> 00:53:13.558
depending on your budget
and what you have access to.

00:53:13.558 --> 00:53:16.560
I don't use cryo, these
cryotherapy chambers,

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:17.393
they're hard to find.

00:53:17.393 --> 00:53:18.330
They're expensive.

00:53:18.330 --> 00:53:21.119
Again, I use sauna and
ice bath and I will do

00:53:21.119 --> 00:53:24.300
anywhere from three to five
rounds, which is a lot,

00:53:24.300 --> 00:53:26.490
anywhere from three to five rounds of heat

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:29.790
for about 20 minutes and
cold for about 5 minutes.

00:53:29.790 --> 00:53:31.290
How cold should the cold be?

00:53:31.290 --> 00:53:34.230
We covered this in the episode
on deliberate cold exposure.

00:53:34.230 --> 00:53:35.790
Here's a general rule of thumb.

00:53:35.790 --> 00:53:39.690
It should be cold enough that
you really want to get out,

00:53:39.690 --> 00:53:41.880
but not so cold that it's unsafe.

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:43.680
And that will vary from person to person.

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:46.260
So I cannot give you a
simple prescriptive there.

00:53:46.260 --> 00:53:47.310
Same thing with the heat,

00:53:47.310 --> 00:53:49.380
hot enough that you're
sweating and that you want

00:53:49.380 --> 00:53:51.870
to get out, but not so hot
that you're running the risk

00:53:51.870 --> 00:53:54.510
of injuring yourself or killing yourself.

00:53:54.510 --> 00:53:56.340
And again, that will vary
from person to person.

00:53:56.340 --> 00:53:57.570
So you have to build up slowly,

00:53:57.570 --> 00:54:00.030
be careful and build up empirically.

00:54:00.030 --> 00:54:01.860
I do that on Tuesdays again as a way

00:54:01.860 --> 00:54:03.778
to accelerate recovery and
because it's very clear

00:54:03.778 --> 00:54:05.790
that there are cardiovascular benefits,

00:54:05.790 --> 00:54:09.420
maybe even benefits for the brain related

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:11.340
to the cardiovascular
benefits because of course

00:54:11.340 --> 00:54:14.040
the brain needs a lot of
blood flow and needs a lot

00:54:14.040 --> 00:54:15.761
of nutrients and other things flowing

00:54:15.761 --> 00:54:17.610
into and out of there,

00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:20.460
debris out and nutrients and
other things into the brain.

00:54:22.380 --> 00:54:25.110
Heat can help accelerate
that or improve that.

00:54:25.110 --> 00:54:27.180
And so I'm doing that to
improve cardiovascular function,

00:54:27.180 --> 00:54:31.440
improve brain health, and
then the cold contrast

00:54:31.440 --> 00:54:34.350
provides a sort of accelerator
on that or an amplifier

00:54:34.350 --> 00:54:35.850
I think is the better way to phrase it

00:54:35.850 --> 00:54:37.530
on that process because in the cold

00:54:37.530 --> 00:54:39.690
you get vasoconstriction
and then in the heat

00:54:39.690 --> 00:54:40.523
you get vasodilation.

00:54:40.523 --> 00:54:42.330
And so you're maximizing that process,

00:54:42.330 --> 00:54:44.820
which is actually a neural process.

00:54:44.820 --> 00:54:46.470
Nerves actually innervate
the blood vessels

00:54:46.470 --> 00:54:48.930
and capillaries and even the arteries

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:50.970
in order to allow that
constriction and dilation

00:54:50.970 --> 00:54:52.440
process to occur.

00:54:52.440 --> 00:54:54.200
So Tuesday is really about recovery,

00:54:54.200 --> 00:54:56.849
but my recovery day isn't necessarily

00:54:56.849 --> 00:55:00.210
about just laying around
and not doing anything.

00:55:00.210 --> 00:55:02.310
I might still also take
some walks that day.

00:55:02.310 --> 00:55:04.200
Remember, I want to try
and get that 200 minutes

00:55:04.200 --> 00:55:06.030
of zone two cardio across the week,

00:55:06.030 --> 00:55:08.310
and sometimes, not often,

00:55:08.310 --> 00:55:10.950
but sometimes I'll get
in a few minutes or more

00:55:10.950 --> 00:55:12.660
of walking quickly that day.

00:55:12.660 --> 00:55:15.060
But generally I'm working
a lot on Tuesday as I do

00:55:15.060 --> 00:55:17.580
on Monday, and I'm a
little bit tired and maybe

00:55:17.580 --> 00:55:19.650
even a little bit sore from my leg workout

00:55:19.650 --> 00:55:20.730
the previous day Monday.

00:55:20.730 --> 00:55:23.220
So I try and get that hot cold contrast.

00:55:23.220 --> 00:55:25.412
There are other benefits
to hot and cold contrast.

00:55:25.412 --> 00:55:28.950
We have a description of
the different protocols

00:55:28.950 --> 00:55:31.650
for hot and for cold and their contrast

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:33.150
at our Huberman Lab newsletter.

00:55:33.150 --> 00:55:35.250
You can find that by
going to hubermanlab.com.

00:55:35.250 --> 00:55:37.050
Go to the newsletter tab under the menu

00:55:37.050 --> 00:55:38.176
and you can sign up.

00:55:38.176 --> 00:55:39.420
You can actually download
those protocols very

00:55:39.420 --> 00:55:41.792
easily without even signing
up if you just want to

00:55:41.792 --> 00:55:43.451
access them straight off.

00:55:43.451 --> 00:55:45.540
So Tuesday is really about
recovery and about getting

00:55:45.540 --> 00:55:47.760
some additional cardiovascular benefits

00:55:47.760 --> 00:55:49.140
from heat cold contrast.

00:55:49.140 --> 00:55:50.793
One other thing that's
built into the rationale

00:55:50.793 --> 00:55:53.940
for doing a lot of heat and
cold on one day as opposed

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:55.080
to doing it every day...

00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:56.640
Well, in addition to it
being a little bit more

00:55:56.640 --> 00:55:58.794
convenient because certainly
some people don't access

00:55:58.794 --> 00:56:03.600
to heat and cold sauna and
cold dunks et cetera every day.

00:56:03.600 --> 00:56:06.030
So maybe getting to do
that one day is more

00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:07.560
accessible or feasible.

00:56:07.560 --> 00:56:08.610
But in addition to that,

00:56:08.610 --> 00:56:12.120
it's very clear that
while there are benefits

00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:14.100
to doing sauna often,

00:56:14.100 --> 00:56:15.540
and we talked about this
in the deliberate heat

00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:17.040
episode and the episode

00:56:17.040 --> 00:56:18.720
with Dr. Rhonda Patrick
when she was a guest

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:22.230
on this podcast, it's
also clear that if you do

00:56:22.230 --> 00:56:24.720
sauna seldom, that is once a week,

00:56:24.720 --> 00:56:27.510
but you do a lot of it on
one day, so in this case,

00:56:27.510 --> 00:56:30.570
it's an hour, if it's,
remember, it's one more,

00:56:30.570 --> 00:56:32.760
it's three to five rounds
of 20 minutes of sauna

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:36.030
followed by about 5 minutes of cold or so,

00:56:36.030 --> 00:56:37.799
by doing that all on one day,

00:56:37.799 --> 00:56:39.870
the peer reviewed research that's covered

00:56:39.870 --> 00:56:42.120
in the episode on deliberate heat,

00:56:42.120 --> 00:56:43.740
this is a study out of Finland,

00:56:43.740 --> 00:56:45.270
showed that you get massive,

00:56:45.270 --> 00:56:47.610
even 16 fold increases in growth hormone,

00:56:47.610 --> 00:56:49.650
which are extremely
beneficial for metabolism

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:51.000
and for recovery.

00:56:51.000 --> 00:56:53.490
So these massive increases
in growth hormone are seen

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:55.689
when you are doing these sessions of sauna

00:56:55.689 --> 00:56:59.040
that are repeated on the
same day and you're only

00:56:59.040 --> 00:57:00.240
doing that about once a week.

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:01.860
Whereas if you do sauna more often,

00:57:01.860 --> 00:57:03.660
there are certainly benefits to that,

00:57:03.660 --> 00:57:07.088
but it's time consuming and
you need access to sauna

00:57:07.088 --> 00:57:08.970
more often than one day
a week if you're doing

00:57:08.970 --> 00:57:10.320
it more than one day a week.

00:57:10.320 --> 00:57:12.090
But if you do it one day a
week and you're doing a lot

00:57:12.090 --> 00:57:15.519
of sessions within that
day, as I've detailed here,

00:57:15.519 --> 00:57:17.805
you see these massive
increases in growth hormone

00:57:17.805 --> 00:57:20.940
that are not observed if
you're doing sauna more often

00:57:20.940 --> 00:57:22.860
for the other benefits of sauna.

00:57:22.860 --> 00:57:24.600
Now the effects of cold are many,

00:57:24.600 --> 00:57:26.730
it's not just vasoconstriction,

00:57:26.730 --> 00:57:29.730
but the effects of cold
are also counterbalanced

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:32.910
by some of the problems with
deliberate cold exposure

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:35.520
that maybe you've heard about
on this podcast and a lot

00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:39.716
of other podcasts and seem
to be a kind of a buzz theme

00:57:39.716 --> 00:57:41.730
on Twitter and elsewhere.

00:57:41.730 --> 00:57:42.630
And the point is this,

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:44.700
there are a number of
quality studies showing

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:46.620
that if you do deliberate cold exposure,

00:57:46.620 --> 00:57:48.870
in particular ice baths
or getting into very cold

00:57:48.870 --> 00:57:53.130
water immediately after an
endurance training session

00:57:53.130 --> 00:57:58.130
or a strength and hypertrophy
session, it can indeed, yes,

00:57:58.170 --> 00:58:01.632
it can disrupt or prevent
some of the adaptations

00:58:01.632 --> 00:58:04.530
that you are seeking with
strength and hypertrophy

00:58:04.530 --> 00:58:06.210
and endurance workouts.

00:58:06.210 --> 00:58:07.860
Okay, so you heard that right.

00:58:07.860 --> 00:58:11.790
And I believe that to be
true based on now several

00:58:11.790 --> 00:58:13.320
quality peer-reviewed studies.

00:58:13.320 --> 00:58:14.850
So by doing your deliberate cold exposure

00:58:14.850 --> 00:58:17.784
on Tuesday, you're not
going to get those effects,

00:58:17.784 --> 00:58:21.926
that is the blocking of
hypertrophy or the blocking

00:58:21.926 --> 00:58:25.110
of strength improvement or
the blocking or prevention

00:58:25.110 --> 00:58:27.120
of improvements in
endurance that would occur

00:58:27.120 --> 00:58:28.699
if you immediately got into the ice bath

00:58:28.699 --> 00:58:32.340
after a hypertrophy strength
or endurance workout.

00:58:32.340 --> 00:58:35.550
Now the caveat to that is if
you are somebody who likes

00:58:35.550 --> 00:58:36.581
to do cold showers,

00:58:36.581 --> 00:58:40.410
I am not aware of any
data that says that cold

00:58:40.410 --> 00:58:43.290
showers cannot be
performed after a strength

00:58:43.290 --> 00:58:45.750
hypertrophy or endurance workout.

00:58:45.750 --> 00:58:49.290
Cold showers are different
than submersion up

00:58:49.290 --> 00:58:52.290
to the neck in an ice
bath or another cold body

00:58:52.290 --> 00:58:54.090
of water for a number
of different reasons.

00:58:54.090 --> 00:58:56.910
In fact, they tap into different aspects

00:58:56.910 --> 00:58:58.260
of the nervous system entirely.

00:58:58.260 --> 00:58:59.460
We don't have time to go into that now,

00:58:59.460 --> 00:59:01.980
it's covered in the episode
on deliberate cold exposure,

00:59:01.980 --> 00:59:04.830
but the simple point is by doing your heat

00:59:04.830 --> 00:59:07.560
and cold contrast, or hey, listen,

00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:09.120
if you're somebody who
doesn't have access to sauna

00:59:09.120 --> 00:59:10.620
or you don't like hot
baths and you just do

00:59:10.620 --> 00:59:14.070
some deliberate cold
exposure on Tuesday, you are

00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:15.837
doing that separate from
your strength and hypertrophy

00:59:15.837 --> 00:59:18.896
and endurance workouts such
that it will not impede

00:59:18.896 --> 00:59:21.210
the benefits of those workouts.

00:59:21.210 --> 00:59:24.600
Okay, so long endurance on
Sunday, leg resistance training

00:59:24.600 --> 00:59:28.230
on Monday and on Tuesday,
heat cold contrast.

00:59:28.230 --> 00:59:29.850
That brings us to Wednesday.

00:59:29.850 --> 00:59:32.160
And on Wednesday we get back

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:34.620
to a resistance training workout.

00:59:34.620 --> 00:59:37.980
And the resistance training
workout that I emphasize

00:59:37.980 --> 00:59:41.460
on Wednesday is one in
which you train your torso.

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:42.660
Yes, literally your torso.

00:59:42.660 --> 00:59:45.690
I know this is counter to
this so-called bro science

00:59:45.690 --> 00:59:46.620
of bro splits.

00:59:46.620 --> 00:59:48.240
I don't know who originated that term,

00:59:48.240 --> 00:59:49.260
it's a terrible term.

00:59:49.260 --> 00:59:53.280
It essentially alienates
anyone who's not a bro

00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:55.170
or considers themselves a bro.

00:59:55.170 --> 00:59:56.550
But in any case,

00:59:56.550 --> 00:59:59.670
this is not about training
chest or back or shoulders.

00:59:59.670 --> 01:00:02.910
In fact, it's really about
strengthening the muscles

01:00:02.910 --> 01:00:05.730
of the torso and of
course includes the chest

01:00:05.730 --> 01:00:07.290
and the shoulders and the back.

01:00:07.290 --> 01:00:08.730
And I'm sure, as I say this,

01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:10.230
a number of people out
there who are obsessed

01:00:10.230 --> 01:00:12.270
with hypertrophy and
muscle growth and filling

01:00:12.270 --> 01:00:15.090
out their shirts or whatever
it may be, are thinking,

01:00:15.090 --> 01:00:17.850
oh no, this is just kind
of all around fitness.

01:00:17.850 --> 01:00:19.620
But no, the point is,

01:00:19.620 --> 01:00:23.700
on Wednesday you train your
torso and that's going to

01:00:23.700 --> 01:00:25.248
involve some pushing, so that's,

01:00:25.248 --> 01:00:29.040
for you that might include
some training of things

01:00:29.040 --> 01:00:31.080
like bench presses or
incline presses as well

01:00:31.080 --> 01:00:33.180
as shoulder presses or lateral raises,

01:00:33.180 --> 01:00:34.890
things for the shoulders
as well as for the back,

01:00:34.890 --> 01:00:36.240
some pulling exercises.

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:38.610
These could be bent over
rows or chin-ups or pull-ups.

01:00:38.610 --> 01:00:41.910
Again, there are enormous
number of exercise for each

01:00:41.910 --> 01:00:43.350
and every one of these muscle groups.

01:00:43.350 --> 01:00:45.900
Now, I believe there's a
clear benefit to training

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:49.020
all these muscle groups
together on the same day,

01:00:49.020 --> 01:00:51.070
because much in the same
way that training legs all

01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:53.700
on one day can lead to
these systemic effects

01:00:53.700 --> 01:00:55.290
because they're large muscle groups,

01:00:55.290 --> 01:00:56.880
working both the pushing
muscles and the pulling

01:00:56.880 --> 01:00:58.680
muscles of the torso on one day,

01:00:58.680 --> 01:01:00.210
at least in the context of this program,

01:01:00.210 --> 01:01:03.810
is very time efficient,
and tends to wick out

01:01:03.810 --> 01:01:05.370
into a number of different dimensions

01:01:05.370 --> 01:01:07.800
of health that at least I'm
interested in and I think

01:01:07.800 --> 01:01:09.030
a lot of other people are interested in.

01:01:09.030 --> 01:01:09.863
What are those?

01:01:09.863 --> 01:01:10.696
Well, let's think again,

01:01:10.696 --> 01:01:14.040
I want to be strong in not just
my legs, but my upper body.

01:01:14.040 --> 01:01:18.600
I also may want, may want
to engage some hypertrophy,

01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:20.910
to grow certain muscle
groups in order to create

01:01:20.910 --> 01:01:21.743
a sense of balance.

01:01:21.743 --> 01:01:22.710
I could be for aesthetic reasons,

01:01:22.710 --> 01:01:24.810
but also for balancing
strength and for health of,

01:01:24.810 --> 01:01:27.000
and the integrity of
the joints, et cetera.

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:28.620
And in addition to that,

01:01:28.620 --> 01:01:31.380
by training a bunch of different
muscle groups together,

01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:33.930
you have the opportunity
to get the more systemic

01:01:33.930 --> 01:01:37.800
hormonal effects and
metabolic effects that occur

01:01:37.800 --> 01:01:39.570
when you're not just
training one muscle group

01:01:39.570 --> 01:01:40.950
and isolating that one muscle group,

01:01:40.950 --> 01:01:43.650
but rather training a bunch
of muscle groups together.

01:01:43.650 --> 01:01:46.050
So Wednesday I train torso
and I do that in push

01:01:46.050 --> 01:01:50.070
pull fashion just for
kind of time efficiency.

01:01:50.070 --> 01:01:52.440
Sometimes that means doing
a pushing exercise and then

01:01:52.440 --> 01:01:53.305
a pulling exercise.

01:01:53.305 --> 01:01:55.581
Sometimes it might even
mean doing a set of pushing

01:01:55.581 --> 01:01:57.720
and then a set of pulling
and going back and forth.

01:01:57.720 --> 01:01:59.370
However, if you're in a gym,

01:01:59.370 --> 01:02:01.320
in particular, a crowded gym,

01:02:01.320 --> 01:02:02.400
please don't be one of those people

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:04.950
that colonizes multiple
pieces of equipment and says,

01:02:04.950 --> 01:02:06.300
I'm working there, I'm working there,

01:02:06.300 --> 01:02:08.250
and that can be quite a
dance and it can be hard

01:02:08.250 --> 01:02:10.110
to orchestrate a workout like that.

01:02:10.110 --> 01:02:12.750
So sometimes it will be
starting off with a set

01:02:12.750 --> 01:02:15.391
of shoulder presses and
then doing all your sets

01:02:15.391 --> 01:02:18.390
of those and then moving
to your chin ups and then

01:02:18.390 --> 01:02:21.630
moving perhaps back to
shoulders and realizing, ah, oh,

01:02:21.630 --> 01:02:23.130
someone's on the machine that I wanted,

01:02:23.130 --> 01:02:24.450
or using the equipment that I wanted,

01:02:24.450 --> 01:02:25.770
so I'll just finish up the pulling,

01:02:25.770 --> 01:02:27.930
I'll finish up the back work
and then go in to the push.

01:02:27.930 --> 01:02:30.690
I don't obsess over the
alternation in any kind

01:02:30.690 --> 01:02:31.620
of strict way.

01:02:31.620 --> 01:02:33.960
I really just try and get the
muscles of the torso trained.

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:36.390
And again, it's two
exercises per muscle group.

01:02:36.390 --> 01:02:39.000
And one of those exercises
is going to be something

01:02:39.000 --> 01:02:40.929
where there's, I realize this isn't

01:02:40.929 --> 01:02:43.470
physiologically accurate, but a shortening

01:02:43.470 --> 01:02:46.616
of the muscle or where they,
at the end of the movement,

01:02:46.616 --> 01:02:50.070
the muscle is under maximal contraction.

01:02:50.070 --> 01:02:51.750
I could throw out some names of exercises

01:02:51.750 --> 01:02:53.520
just for purpose of understanding.

01:02:53.520 --> 01:02:57.270
So this would be like cable
crossovers for the chest,

01:02:57.270 --> 01:03:00.030
the peak contraction is at the end,

01:03:00.030 --> 01:03:01.830
whereas something like an incline press,

01:03:01.830 --> 01:03:03.390
there's more of a stretch
provided it's done

01:03:03.390 --> 01:03:05.610
over a full range of
motion at the beginning

01:03:05.610 --> 01:03:06.443
of the movement.

01:03:06.443 --> 01:03:07.380
So again, something
where there's a stretch

01:03:07.380 --> 01:03:08.880
and something where
there's a peak contraction.

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:10.710
For the shoulders, it's a
little bit harder to do,

01:03:10.710 --> 01:03:11.730
although there are ways to do that.

01:03:11.730 --> 01:03:14.654
And Jeff Cavaliere has
excellent workouts available,

01:03:14.654 --> 01:03:16.350
zero cost, on YouTube.

01:03:16.350 --> 01:03:19.620
He also has excellent programs
on his athleanx.com site.

01:03:19.620 --> 01:03:22.680
but certainly has a lot
of excellent protocols

01:03:22.680 --> 01:03:24.240
on his YouTube and Instagram.

01:03:24.240 --> 01:03:28.650
But on YouTube you can
put in his name and any

01:03:28.650 --> 01:03:29.820
muscle group that you want to train.

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:33.123
And he has some terrific
videos describing exercise

01:03:33.123 --> 01:03:36.360
choice and other features
of exercise parameters.

01:03:36.360 --> 01:03:40.950
Again, a peak contraction
or shortening of the muscle,

01:03:40.950 --> 01:03:43.047
peak contraction exercise
and a stretching exercise.

01:03:43.047 --> 01:03:46.530
And so for the back, one might say, okay,

01:03:46.530 --> 01:03:50.460
a seated row or a bent
over row or a dumbbell row

01:03:50.460 --> 01:03:52.320
where the elbow is
brought behind the torso

01:03:52.320 --> 01:03:54.150
for a peak contraction movement.

01:03:54.150 --> 01:03:56.400
And then for more of a
stretching movement might

01:03:56.400 --> 01:03:58.950
be something like a chin up or a pull-up.

01:03:58.950 --> 01:03:59.790
And as I say this,

01:03:59.790 --> 01:04:03.090
I understand that stretching
and peak contraction

01:04:03.090 --> 01:04:05.755
aren't the exact terms that
one would use if they were

01:04:05.755 --> 01:04:08.670
a physiotherapist or a strength
and conditioning coach,

01:04:08.670 --> 01:04:11.130
but I think for the
typical person who's trying

01:04:11.130 --> 01:04:13.530
to generate strength and
hypertrophy in those muscles

01:04:13.530 --> 01:04:15.717
or maintain strength and
hypertrophy in those muscles,

01:04:15.717 --> 01:04:18.180
this kind of nomenclature
way of describing

01:04:18.180 --> 01:04:21.900
it at least should be
clear and even efficient.

01:04:21.900 --> 01:04:25.410
And just to remind you,
as with the leg workout,

01:04:25.410 --> 01:04:28.710
the total duration of the
torso workout is going to be

01:04:28.710 --> 01:04:31.470
50 to 60 minutes after a brief warmup.

01:04:31.470 --> 01:04:34.950
The sets and repetitions
are going to be dictated

01:04:34.950 --> 01:04:36.480
in the same way that I described earlier.

01:04:36.480 --> 01:04:40.620
So for about a month it's
going to be more sets.

01:04:40.620 --> 01:04:42.390
So anywhere from three to five sets

01:04:42.390 --> 01:04:43.890
in the lower repetition range,

01:04:43.890 --> 01:04:46.170
so four to eight repetitions,

01:04:46.170 --> 01:04:48.600
so that's going to be heavier
weights and longer rest

01:04:48.600 --> 01:04:50.520
as I described earlier,
the rest intervals.

01:04:50.520 --> 01:04:52.130
And then for the next
month it's going to be

01:04:52.130 --> 01:04:54.504
moderate repetitions, fewer sets,

01:04:54.504 --> 01:04:56.049
the same way I described earlier.

01:04:56.049 --> 01:04:58.800
And if you want more
details on all of that,

01:04:58.800 --> 01:05:01.560
you can find that in
the newsletter related

01:05:01.560 --> 01:05:04.470
to the optimal or
foundational fitness protocol

01:05:04.470 --> 01:05:06.420
that you can access at hubermanlab.com.

01:05:06.420 --> 01:05:08.460
One thing I should note
about the Wednesday torso

01:05:08.460 --> 01:05:11.224
workout is that I am a
big believer in training

01:05:11.224 --> 01:05:15.210
the, what I believe is the highly avoided,

01:05:15.210 --> 01:05:18.366
or at least overlooked but
vitally important aspect

01:05:18.366 --> 01:05:22.110
of total body stability,
strength and safety, really,

01:05:22.110 --> 01:05:23.547
safety, which is the neck.

01:05:23.547 --> 01:05:26.070
Now I realize a lot of people
don't want a large neck,

01:05:26.070 --> 01:05:27.990
and I totally understand
for aesthetic reasons

01:05:27.990 --> 01:05:29.280
why they don't want that.

01:05:29.280 --> 01:05:31.050
It's kind of interesting
actually, if you think about it,

01:05:31.050 --> 01:05:33.150
that people who have a
large neck are often told

01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:34.260
they have no neck.

01:05:34.260 --> 01:05:37.110
People say that guy has no
neck or they have no neck

01:05:37.110 --> 01:05:38.580
when in fact they're referring to the fact

01:05:38.580 --> 01:05:39.660
that they have a very large neck.

01:05:39.660 --> 01:05:40.500
I don't know how that came to be.

01:05:40.500 --> 01:05:42.240
Somebody put in the comments why that is,

01:05:42.240 --> 01:05:44.130
how come when people have
a big neck they refer

01:05:44.130 --> 01:05:45.600
to it as no neck.

01:05:45.600 --> 01:05:46.590
So why do I train the neck?

01:05:46.590 --> 01:05:48.270
I train the neck for a couple of reasons.

01:05:48.270 --> 01:05:50.460
One is years ago I had an
accent where I actually fell

01:05:50.460 --> 01:05:53.710
off a roof and I'd been
training my neck at that time

01:05:55.080 --> 01:05:57.360
for a sport that I was
involved in and I walked

01:05:57.360 --> 01:05:59.370
away from it with a sore
neck but not a broken neck.

01:05:59.370 --> 01:06:01.820
And I thought, wow, it's
really great that I have

01:06:02.670 --> 01:06:03.810
been training my neck.

01:06:03.810 --> 01:06:04.770
In addition to that,

01:06:04.770 --> 01:06:08.012
I was once in a car accident
where I was parked...

01:06:08.012 --> 01:06:11.490
I just bought the car, was
my first new car purchased,

01:06:11.490 --> 01:06:13.980
parked in that car with my mother,

01:06:13.980 --> 01:06:16.890
my grandfather in the
backseat at the red light

01:06:16.890 --> 01:06:18.840
and someone rammed into us at full speed.

01:06:18.840 --> 01:06:22.533
Now fortunately, none of us
were hurt. We were all rattled.

01:06:23.430 --> 01:06:27.510
And once again, I was very
sore in my back and in my neck.

01:06:27.510 --> 01:06:30.210
But I think one of the
reasons why I was able

01:06:30.210 --> 01:06:31.710
to essentially walk away from that,

01:06:31.710 --> 01:06:34.320
I didn't have any sustained damage was

01:06:34.320 --> 01:06:35.430
because I trained my neck,

01:06:35.430 --> 01:06:38.430
but I started training my
neck for sport and I continue

01:06:38.430 --> 01:06:40.380
to train my neck because I
notice when I don't train

01:06:40.380 --> 01:06:42.210
my neck I start getting shoulder issues.

01:06:42.210 --> 01:06:45.690
And if you talk to an
excellent physiologist like

01:06:45.690 --> 01:06:48.540
Dr. Kelly Starrett of The Ready State,

01:06:48.540 --> 01:06:49.938
is an excellent channel,

01:06:49.938 --> 01:06:50.910
you can find them on all the social media

01:06:50.910 --> 01:06:53.796
and standard channels
or you talk to anyone

01:06:53.796 --> 01:06:56.520
out there who really
understands the strength

01:06:56.520 --> 01:06:59.010
of the torso and the upper
body and even the back.

01:06:59.010 --> 01:07:01.200
What you learn is that, of course,

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:03.240
being the upper portion of the spine,

01:07:03.240 --> 01:07:05.400
stabilizing your neck is very important.

01:07:05.400 --> 01:07:07.740
Now, training the neck can
be a little bit detailed

01:07:07.740 --> 01:07:10.290
and specific and even
dangerous if you do it wrong.

01:07:10.290 --> 01:07:12.600
Again, Jeff Cavaliere has
a terrific set of videos

01:07:12.600 --> 01:07:13.800
on training the neck properly.

01:07:13.800 --> 01:07:15.660
I know a lot of people
out there might think neck

01:07:15.660 --> 01:07:17.730
bridges and I used to do neck bridges.

01:07:17.730 --> 01:07:21.240
I occasionally still sneak in
a neck bridge here or there,

01:07:21.240 --> 01:07:22.848
although I don't recommend
it because in discussions

01:07:22.848 --> 01:07:25.885
with Jeff, he will tell
you, and it's true,

01:07:25.885 --> 01:07:29.340
that the discs eventually
go and you can run

01:07:29.340 --> 01:07:31.260
into serious issues from doing bridges

01:07:31.260 --> 01:07:33.180
and it doesn't happen
gradually so you can't

01:07:33.180 --> 01:07:34.013
notice it happening.

01:07:34.013 --> 01:07:35.220
It just happens suddenly.

01:07:35.220 --> 01:07:37.590
So I might occasionally do a neck bridge,

01:07:37.590 --> 01:07:40.200
but in general I'll train
neck by wrapping a plate

01:07:40.200 --> 01:07:42.090
in a towel so that I don't
end up with an imprint

01:07:42.090 --> 01:07:44.538
of the weight value on my head or face.

01:07:44.538 --> 01:07:47.430
And then moving the neck
from side to side or front

01:07:47.430 --> 01:07:50.610
or back, and again, we'll
provide a link to those videos.

01:07:50.610 --> 01:07:52.260
It's a terrific set of
videos that describe

01:07:52.260 --> 01:07:54.300
how to train your neck
properly and safely.

01:07:54.300 --> 01:07:56.276
So even if you're not
trying to grow your neck,

01:07:56.276 --> 01:07:57.930
you definitely want to
make sure that you use

01:07:57.930 --> 01:07:59.880
some light weights to
make sure that your neck

01:07:59.880 --> 01:08:01.647
is stable and upright.

01:08:01.647 --> 01:08:04.800
And I say stable and upright
because it's very clear

01:08:04.800 --> 01:08:08.250
that for reasons related
to texting and staring down

01:08:08.250 --> 01:08:11.670
at computers and related
to weak neck relative

01:08:11.670 --> 01:08:14.012
to the rest of the muscles
that stabilize the spine,

01:08:14.012 --> 01:08:16.140
a lot of people,

01:08:16.140 --> 01:08:18.450
their default stance or
their default posture

01:08:18.450 --> 01:08:21.540
is with chin forward and that's not good.

01:08:21.540 --> 01:08:24.540
Not only is it aesthetically not good,

01:08:24.540 --> 01:08:26.670
but it also can create all
sorts of issues related

01:08:26.670 --> 01:08:28.740
to back pain and headaches
and things of that sort.

01:08:28.740 --> 01:08:29.910
This is a real thing,

01:08:29.910 --> 01:08:31.320
training your neck allows you to stand

01:08:31.320 --> 01:08:32.670
upright, sit upright.

01:08:32.670 --> 01:08:34.770
I even believe that it
allows you to do things like

01:08:34.770 --> 01:08:36.600
public speaking or have
conversations with people

01:08:36.600 --> 01:08:38.970
on the street in a way
where you are front facing

01:08:38.970 --> 01:08:40.350
as opposed to looking down.

01:08:40.350 --> 01:08:44.430
So Wednesday is torso
and neck and then comes

01:08:44.430 --> 01:08:48.240
Thursday and that means
another cardiovascular

01:08:48.240 --> 01:08:52.080
exercise session,
although it's a brief one.

01:08:52.080 --> 01:08:53.820
Unlike the endurance training

01:08:53.820 --> 01:08:55.950
on Sunday, the cardiovascular session

01:08:55.950 --> 01:08:58.110
on Thursday, and again,

01:08:58.110 --> 01:09:00.210
for me it falls on Thursday,
but for it could fall

01:09:00.210 --> 01:09:02.040
on a different day depending
on when you started

01:09:02.040 --> 01:09:06.390
this protocol, is going
to be about, again,

01:09:06.390 --> 01:09:10.122
about 35 minutes of, for me, running,

01:09:10.122 --> 01:09:12.750
although it could be rowing
or it could be cycling,

01:09:12.750 --> 01:09:14.670
it could be something of that sort.

01:09:14.670 --> 01:09:16.680
The goal of this workout
is what's important.

01:09:16.680 --> 01:09:18.810
The goal of this workout is to tap into,

01:09:18.810 --> 01:09:20.490
remember that long list
that we talked about earlier

01:09:20.490 --> 01:09:22.080
where you've got skill and speed and power

01:09:22.080 --> 01:09:23.370
and strength and hypertrophy, et cetera,

01:09:23.370 --> 01:09:24.600
different forms of endurance,

01:09:24.600 --> 01:09:27.270
is to get into that range of endurance

01:09:27.270 --> 01:09:30.540
where your heart rate is
elevated quite a bit more

01:09:30.540 --> 01:09:32.040
than zone two,

01:09:32.040 --> 01:09:34.950
but that you're not really
going all out sprint.

01:09:34.950 --> 01:09:37.650
So what that means for me
is warming up for about 5

01:09:37.650 --> 01:09:38.483
to 10 minutes.

01:09:38.483 --> 01:09:40.710
That could be jogging, a little
bit of light calisthenics,

01:09:40.710 --> 01:09:42.450
might even be hopping
on a stationary bike,

01:09:42.450 --> 01:09:44.670
although to be honest I
loathe the stationary bike,

01:09:44.670 --> 01:09:48.750
and then setting a timer
and doing about 30,

01:09:48.750 --> 01:09:53.430
but ideally 35 minutes of what I call

01:09:53.430 --> 01:09:56.160
75% to 80% of all out.

01:09:56.160 --> 01:09:59.010
Okay, now I realize this
spits in the face of all

01:09:59.010 --> 01:10:04.010
you heart rate monitor wearing
super techy exercise types.

01:10:04.470 --> 01:10:09.357
But when I think of all out
sprint, I think of 100%.

01:10:09.357 --> 01:10:10.350
And what is that?

01:10:10.350 --> 01:10:12.480
In my mind that's somebody is chasing me

01:10:12.480 --> 01:10:15.630
with a needle full of
poison and I am sprinting

01:10:15.630 --> 01:10:17.730
away at maximal speed.

01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:19.500
That for me is 100%.

01:10:19.500 --> 01:10:20.760
So after a brief warm up,

01:10:20.760 --> 01:10:23.550
what I'm going to do is
go out, typically outside,

01:10:23.550 --> 01:10:25.230
although sometimes it
has to be on a treadmill

01:10:25.230 --> 01:10:30.230
if I'm traveling, and move,
run for about 30 to 35 minutes

01:10:30.540 --> 01:10:34.110
at about 75% or 80% of that all out.

01:10:34.110 --> 01:10:37.449
What that means is that I'm
striving to keep a steady pace,

01:10:37.449 --> 01:10:39.905
but in reality I don't.

01:10:39.905 --> 01:10:42.600
I sometimes have to stop at
a stoplight, there are cars,

01:10:42.600 --> 01:10:44.134
please don't run into
traffic just to maintain

01:10:44.134 --> 01:10:45.930
that speed and that timing,

01:10:45.930 --> 01:10:50.610
that would be terribly
antagonistic to fitness,

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:52.053
in particular, lifespan.

01:10:53.040 --> 01:10:54.930
That running tends to be running

01:10:54.930 --> 01:10:56.970
in which I'm breathing
hard so I'm not able

01:10:56.970 --> 01:10:59.070
to restrict myself to
purely nasal breathing.

01:10:59.070 --> 01:11:00.390
And I should have mentioned earlier,

01:11:00.390 --> 01:11:05.390
on the Sunday long ruck
or weighted hike or jog,

01:11:05.550 --> 01:11:07.934
if I'm alone, I try and
do pure nasal breathing.

01:11:07.934 --> 01:11:10.380
If I'm with other people or I'm talking,

01:11:10.380 --> 01:11:12.630
obviously I'm not going
to do pure nasal breathing

01:11:12.630 --> 01:11:15.300
because I'm talking, although
I'm sure that sometimes

01:11:15.300 --> 01:11:17.010
they wish I was doing
pure nasal breathing.

01:11:17.010 --> 01:11:19.350
That Thursday workout
accomplishes a number of things.

01:11:19.350 --> 01:11:22.071
First of all, it really
gets my heart rate up

01:11:22.071 --> 01:11:25.320
and it improves multiple
aspects of endurance,

01:11:25.320 --> 01:11:27.120
because as you recall earlier,

01:11:27.120 --> 01:11:29.220
the different bins of
endurance that include

01:11:29.220 --> 01:11:31.710
muscular endurance, anaerobic,
that 3 to 12 minute range

01:11:31.710 --> 01:11:33.420
and then 30 minutes or longer,

01:11:33.420 --> 01:11:36.060
none of them really precisely
match what's accomplished

01:11:36.060 --> 01:11:40.320
in this 35 minute or so
cardiovascular session

01:11:40.320 --> 01:11:43.053
where I'm pushing hard but not all out.

01:11:44.370 --> 01:11:46.080
But that's exactly the reason to do it,

01:11:46.080 --> 01:11:49.050
which is that it taps
into multiple fuel systems

01:11:49.050 --> 01:11:52.950
for the muscle and multiple
aspects of the heart

01:11:52.950 --> 01:11:54.510
and capillaries and
arteries and veins that are

01:11:54.510 --> 01:11:56.160
involved in generating that movement.

01:11:56.160 --> 01:11:58.260
So it really cuts a
broad swath into multiple

01:11:58.260 --> 01:11:59.490
categories of endurance.

01:11:59.490 --> 01:12:02.580
And also just keep in mind
what this foundational

01:12:02.580 --> 01:12:06.060
or optimal fitness protocol
is really designed to do.

01:12:06.060 --> 01:12:09.540
In my mind, a foundational
fitness protocol is one

01:12:09.540 --> 01:12:13.680
that leaves you or has you
in a state where if you need

01:12:13.680 --> 01:12:15.690
to walk really far and
carry a bunch of weight,

01:12:15.690 --> 01:12:16.710
you can do it.

01:12:16.710 --> 01:12:18.870
If you need to lift a heavy
object with your legs,

01:12:18.870 --> 01:12:19.860
you can do it.

01:12:19.860 --> 01:12:23.970
If you need to run really
fast for two minutes,

01:12:23.970 --> 01:12:25.020
you can do it.

01:12:25.020 --> 01:12:28.860
And if you need to run
a little bit further,

01:12:28.860 --> 01:12:31.110
like maybe in 10 minutes
for whatever reason,

01:12:31.110 --> 01:12:31.943
you can do that.

01:12:31.943 --> 01:12:34.560
So it's a really kind of
all around fitness program

01:12:34.560 --> 01:12:36.600
and that 35 minute run, again,

01:12:36.600 --> 01:12:39.270
could be swapped with a 35 minute erg row.

01:12:39.270 --> 01:12:41.100
Or sometimes if you only have access

01:12:41.100 --> 01:12:42.865
to a stationary bike, you could do that.

01:12:42.865 --> 01:12:45.240
I suppose if you didn't
have access to any equipment

01:12:45.240 --> 01:12:48.420
and running is not your thing,
one thing that I have done,

01:12:48.420 --> 01:12:49.980
especially if I've been
stuck in a hotel 'cause

01:12:49.980 --> 01:12:51.690
I arrived late someplace
and I really want to get

01:12:51.690 --> 01:12:54.540
this workout in, you could
do the dreaded burpee.

01:12:54.540 --> 01:12:56.040
I know there are a lot
of opinions out there,

01:12:56.040 --> 01:12:57.780
some people think burpees
are downright dangerous,

01:12:57.780 --> 01:12:59.370
other people love burpees.

01:12:59.370 --> 01:13:00.557
You could do that.

01:13:00.557 --> 01:13:04.292
Or you could do really fast
but full jumping jacks.

01:13:04.292 --> 01:13:07.530
I know that's a little PE class, right?

01:13:07.530 --> 01:13:09.265
Physical education class-ish.

01:13:09.265 --> 01:13:11.640
But sometimes if I need
to get the workout in,

01:13:11.640 --> 01:13:14.070
what I'll do in a hotel
if I've arrived late

01:13:14.070 --> 01:13:16.980
in particular day of travel
is I will find the stairwell,

01:13:16.980 --> 01:13:18.690
the fire stairwell, I'll
make sure by the way

01:13:18.690 --> 01:13:20.610
that I can get back into the
building 'cause I've been

01:13:20.610 --> 01:13:22.290
locked in those stairwells before.

01:13:22.290 --> 01:13:25.140
And I will simply walk
really fast up the stairwell

01:13:25.140 --> 01:13:26.880
as many flights of stairs as there are,

01:13:26.880 --> 01:13:29.353
or maybe even jog it, not quite sprint,

01:13:29.353 --> 01:13:32.670
but a run up those stairs
over and over and over again

01:13:32.670 --> 01:13:35.551
in order to get that 35
minutes of 75% to 80%

01:13:35.551 --> 01:13:38.970
of max output cardiovascular work done.

01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:41.562
And if I'm really just
restricted to my hotel room,

01:13:41.562 --> 01:13:44.220
I'll just do jumping
jacks for 30, 35 minutes,

01:13:44.220 --> 01:13:45.900
sometimes while watching something on TV.

01:13:45.900 --> 01:13:47.790
And believe me, if you're
doing full jumping jacks,

01:13:47.790 --> 01:13:49.410
like really extending your legs,

01:13:49.410 --> 01:13:51.167
really getting arms
overhead and really doing

01:13:51.167 --> 01:13:54.043
the full movement, by the
time you hit five or six

01:13:54.043 --> 01:13:57.750
minutes you are going to be
sweating and your heart rate

01:13:57.750 --> 01:13:58.740
is really going to be up.

01:13:58.740 --> 01:14:00.450
I also sometimes will
travel with a jump rope.

01:14:00.450 --> 01:14:04.020
I always try and travel with
a jump rope and skip rope,

01:14:04.020 --> 01:14:05.940
much to the dismay of
the people who are housed

01:14:05.940 --> 01:14:07.290
below me in the hotel room.

01:14:07.290 --> 01:14:08.580
Skipping rope, I should mention,

01:14:08.580 --> 01:14:10.590
can be a very effective way of getting

01:14:10.590 --> 01:14:12.930
cardiovascular training
while you're on the road.

01:14:12.930 --> 01:14:14.070
But in all seriousness,

01:14:14.070 --> 01:14:15.870
if you're in a hotel room or an apartment

01:14:15.870 --> 01:14:18.000
and you can't really jump
high and you're very good

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:19.860
at jumping rope, what you'll
find is it's not going to

01:14:19.860 --> 01:14:24.090
get you into that higher
elevated heart rate zone.

01:14:24.090 --> 01:14:24.923
Okay?

01:14:24.923 --> 01:14:25.980
It can be great for
zone two type training,

01:14:25.980 --> 01:14:27.414
but if you're really
good at skipping rope,

01:14:27.414 --> 01:14:29.430
and I wouldn't say I'm really good at it,

01:14:29.430 --> 01:14:31.200
but I've done enough skipping
rope that I can just kind

01:14:31.200 --> 01:14:34.110
of cruise and talk and
it, it's more zone twoish,

01:14:34.110 --> 01:14:35.610
even feels like walking at times.

01:14:35.610 --> 01:14:36.720
Now you can do double unders

01:14:36.720 --> 01:14:38.850
where you're really jumping
and putting the rope

01:14:38.850 --> 01:14:41.400
under you twice each time
or crossovers, et cetera,

01:14:41.400 --> 01:14:43.140
depending on your skill level.

01:14:43.140 --> 01:14:45.060
But again, if you're in an
apartment or you're in a hotel,

01:14:45.060 --> 01:14:46.061
that's going to be harder to do.

01:14:46.061 --> 01:14:48.740
And because there's some skill involved,

01:14:48.740 --> 01:14:50.760
sometimes you're stopping more often

01:14:50.760 --> 01:14:52.140
than you're continuing.

01:14:52.140 --> 01:14:53.819
By the way, and I just
have to mention this,

01:14:53.819 --> 01:14:56.970
a really terrific Instagram
channel is @anna.skips.

01:14:56.970 --> 01:14:58.890
This is a teacher, a science teacher,

01:14:58.890 --> 01:15:00.660
or I believe it's a math,

01:15:00.660 --> 01:15:03.390
maths as they say in the
UK 'cause she's in the UK,

01:15:03.390 --> 01:15:04.223
maths teacher.

01:15:04.223 --> 01:15:06.750
I don't know Anna, but I
know she skips 'cause she has

01:15:06.750 --> 01:15:08.850
this amazing Instagram
channel called Anna Skips.

01:15:08.850 --> 01:15:09.780
And what's really cool

01:15:09.780 --> 01:15:12.900
about her Instagram is she
shows you her progression

01:15:12.900 --> 01:15:14.280
from not being able to skip rope at all

01:15:14.280 --> 01:15:16.980
to the absolutely incredible
types of rope skipping

01:15:16.980 --> 01:15:18.780
that she's doing each morning
while getting sunlight,

01:15:18.780 --> 01:15:21.480
which of course is
essential health protocol.

01:15:21.480 --> 01:15:24.340
So check out Anna Skips on
Instagram, really inspiring

01:15:24.340 --> 01:15:27.360
and made me want to get
better at skipping rope.

01:15:27.360 --> 01:15:28.500
I'm still working at it.

01:15:28.500 --> 01:15:30.930
Okay, so with that
Thursday cardiovascular,

01:15:30.930 --> 01:15:31.920
let's call it endurance,

01:15:31.920 --> 01:15:34.560
but cardiovascular training workout done,

01:15:34.560 --> 01:15:37.664
around roles Friday and
on Friday I'm going to do

01:15:37.664 --> 01:15:40.530
another cardiovascular training session,

01:15:40.530 --> 01:15:41.700
and I alluded to this earlier,

01:15:41.700 --> 01:15:44.010
but this cardiovascular
training session is also

01:15:44.010 --> 01:15:49.010
designed to tap into some
of the ability of hard,

01:15:51.017 --> 01:15:53.850
I should say high intensity
interval training,

01:15:53.850 --> 01:15:57.480
to tap into strength and
hypertrophy increases

01:15:57.480 --> 01:15:58.313
for the legs.

01:15:58.313 --> 01:15:59.880
'Cause remember, we train legs

01:15:59.880 --> 01:16:03.750
on Monday and what the science tells us

01:16:03.750 --> 01:16:06.180
is that protein synthesis
in a muscle group can

01:16:06.180 --> 01:16:09.510
be stimulated about every 42 to 72 hours.

01:16:09.510 --> 01:16:11.880
And so we've had Tuesday off,

01:16:11.880 --> 01:16:15.900
Wednesday off and Thursday
off and you don't want to

01:16:15.900 --> 01:16:19.020
lose progress that you
made from that terrific

01:16:19.020 --> 01:16:20.700
Monday leg workout.

01:16:20.700 --> 01:16:23.370
But in order to make sure
that you can do the other

01:16:23.370 --> 01:16:25.766
things that follow in this
program and pick back up

01:16:25.766 --> 01:16:28.380
on Monday with another leg workout,

01:16:28.380 --> 01:16:30.480
at least for me with my recovery abilities

01:16:30.480 --> 01:16:31.830
and my work schedule,

01:16:31.830 --> 01:16:34.410
I'm not going to do an
entire other leg workout

01:16:34.410 --> 01:16:37.200
because it's going to set
the whole thing out of whack.

01:16:37.200 --> 01:16:38.910
That is, I won't be
able to consistently do

01:16:38.910 --> 01:16:41.010
the same workouts on the
same days of each week.

01:16:41.010 --> 01:16:42.030
Now with that said,

01:16:42.030 --> 01:16:43.440
a little bit later I'll
explain what happens

01:16:43.440 --> 01:16:45.197
if you have to miss a workout
and how you can combine

01:16:45.197 --> 01:16:46.350
days, et cetera.

01:16:46.350 --> 01:16:50.160
But I really strive to
get certain workouts done

01:16:50.160 --> 01:16:52.740
on certain days consistently
at least as best I can.

01:16:52.740 --> 01:16:55.566
So Friday is high intensity
interval training,

01:16:55.566 --> 01:16:57.665
and that can take a
variety of different forms.

01:16:57.665 --> 01:17:01.650
For me, the ideal thing
to do, for me, again,

01:17:01.650 --> 01:17:03.750
you could do something
completely different.

01:17:03.750 --> 01:17:05.970
Exercise choice, again, should be governed

01:17:05.970 --> 01:17:08.538
by what you can do safely
so you don't injure yourself

01:17:08.538 --> 01:17:11.190
and that you can perform
effectively and that gets

01:17:11.190 --> 01:17:13.710
you or provides you the
stimulus that you want.

01:17:13.710 --> 01:17:16.230
And what I'm trying to do
on Friday is get my heart

01:17:16.230 --> 01:17:17.580
rate way, way up.

01:17:17.580 --> 01:17:20.070
Talked about this in the
episode with Dr. Andy Galpin.

01:17:20.070 --> 01:17:23.880
In addition to the benefits
of getting 180-200 minutes

01:17:23.880 --> 01:17:27.000
of zone two cardio per week, minimum,

01:17:27.000 --> 01:17:30.570
it's a really good idea to
get up to that max or near max

01:17:30.570 --> 01:17:32.283
heart rate at least once a week.

01:17:32.283 --> 01:17:34.680
And you're not going to do that
for very long periods of time.

01:17:34.680 --> 01:17:35.880
You're not going to do
that for 30 minutes.

01:17:35.880 --> 01:17:37.770
You can't sprint all out for 30 minutes

01:17:37.770 --> 01:17:39.630
unless you're Steve Prefontaine.

01:17:39.630 --> 01:17:40.463
If you haven't seen

01:17:40.463 --> 01:17:42.297
the movies "Without
Limits" or "Prefontaine,"

01:17:42.297 --> 01:17:44.040
you should absolutely see those.

01:17:44.040 --> 01:17:47.340
He was able to go out and run 12 laps,

01:17:47.340 --> 01:17:50.940
what seemed to be an all
out sprint or close to it.

01:17:50.940 --> 01:17:51.773
Incredible.

01:17:51.773 --> 01:17:54.780
But most people are not
going to do that or going

01:17:54.780 --> 01:17:57.540
be carried away on a
stretcher if they try.

01:17:57.540 --> 01:17:59.760
These high intensity
interval training for me

01:17:59.760 --> 01:18:02.910
ideally would be on so-called
assault bike or Airdyne bikes.

01:18:02.910 --> 01:18:05.610
So these bikes that have the
fan, which might seem like,

01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:06.990
oh, just cools you off,

01:18:06.990 --> 01:18:08.880
but actually there's a
lot of resistance there.

01:18:08.880 --> 01:18:13.020
So what I will typically
do is a 20 to 30 second

01:18:13.020 --> 01:18:17.790
all out sprint using arms and
legs and then 10 seconds rest

01:18:17.790 --> 01:18:20.830
and then repeat all out sprint for 20

01:18:20.830 --> 01:18:24.120
to 30 seconds, 10 seconds rest, repeat.

01:18:24.120 --> 01:18:29.120
And I'll do that for anywhere
from 8 to 12 rounds, which,

01:18:29.460 --> 01:18:32.760
trust me, even if you start
out a little bit less,

01:18:32.760 --> 01:18:35.400
or I should say not all
out intensity or effort,

01:18:35.400 --> 01:18:37.080
by the time you hit
the fifth or sixth one,

01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:41.160
you will be certainly
headed into if not near

01:18:41.160 --> 01:18:42.330
your maximum heart rate.

01:18:42.330 --> 01:18:43.470
Now what is your maximum heart rate?

01:18:43.470 --> 01:18:45.060
Do you need a heart rate monitor? No.

01:18:45.060 --> 01:18:47.280
If you like using that
sort of thing, great.

01:18:47.280 --> 01:18:50.430
But again, Andy Galpin
beautifully supplied us

01:18:50.430 --> 01:18:51.420
with the information.

01:18:51.420 --> 01:18:54.000
He said if you take the
number 220 and you subtract

01:18:54.000 --> 01:18:56.940
your age, that for most people, most,

01:18:56.940 --> 01:18:58.410
is going to be your maximum heart rate.

01:18:58.410 --> 01:19:00.120
Although for certain
people who are very fit

01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:02.070
or certain ages, that's
not going to apply.

01:19:02.070 --> 01:19:04.200
So it's a little bit too crude to measure,

01:19:04.200 --> 01:19:05.847
but it's a good starting
place and you can look up

01:19:05.847 --> 01:19:08.250
other information or see
that podcast episode,

01:19:08.250 --> 01:19:10.050
we provide the link to it
in the show note captions

01:19:10.050 --> 01:19:12.090
if you want to get more details on that.

01:19:12.090 --> 01:19:13.380
I don't use a heart rate monitor.

01:19:13.380 --> 01:19:15.240
What I'm trying to do is get to that point

01:19:15.240 --> 01:19:18.240
where I quote unquote
feel like I want to die.

01:19:18.240 --> 01:19:21.360
Now I don't want to die,
and please don't die, right?

01:19:21.360 --> 01:19:23.040
If you're not in good
cardiovascular health,

01:19:23.040 --> 01:19:26.070
do not just jump right
into this fitness protocol.

01:19:26.070 --> 01:19:28.320
But I want to get to the
point where I really feel like

01:19:28.320 --> 01:19:31.924
I could not pedal any
faster or pull any faster

01:19:31.924 --> 01:19:34.800
on the assault bike, the Airdyne bike,

01:19:34.800 --> 01:19:38.250
or if I'm doing this workout
in a place or at a time

01:19:38.250 --> 01:19:41.880
or because I choose to
not use a bike or a rower,

01:19:41.880 --> 01:19:43.170
'cause you could also use a rower,

01:19:43.170 --> 01:19:46.440
I will simply do sprint jog intervals.

01:19:46.440 --> 01:19:48.408
I will sprint for 20 or 30 seconds,

01:19:48.408 --> 01:19:51.720
then jog for 10 seconds,
sprint for 20 or 30 seconds,

01:19:51.720 --> 01:19:53.970
and then jog for 10
seconds and just repeat.

01:19:53.970 --> 01:19:56.490
I used to have a big field
next to my laboratory,

01:19:56.490 --> 01:19:58.560
my old laboratory, and I
used to bring my bulldog

01:19:58.560 --> 01:19:59.393
Costello out there.

01:19:59.393 --> 01:20:01.655
He was really good at the
first sprint part and then

01:20:01.655 --> 01:20:02.850
he would just lie down and watch.

01:20:02.850 --> 01:20:04.200
he didn't even do the jog part.

01:20:04.200 --> 01:20:05.610
I would just go back and
forth, back and forth,

01:20:05.610 --> 01:20:08.880
back and forth, panting
like a bulldog nonstop,

01:20:08.880 --> 01:20:11.817
barely able to recover
before sprinting again.

01:20:11.817 --> 01:20:14.460
And the basis of this workout
again is several fold.

01:20:14.460 --> 01:20:16.740
First of all, it's to get
the heart rate really high,

01:20:16.740 --> 01:20:19.950
up towards maximum heart
rate at least once a week.

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:21.210
So you accomplish that this Friday.

01:20:21.210 --> 01:20:24.270
Also, if you are
sprinting and then jogging

01:20:24.270 --> 01:20:26.610
or you are really pushing
hard on an assault bike

01:20:26.610 --> 01:20:29.130
or an Airdyne bike, or
using a, for instance,

01:20:29.130 --> 01:20:31.890
a skier or a skier machine or any number

01:20:31.890 --> 01:20:36.120
of different cardiovascular
training tools,

01:20:36.120 --> 01:20:40.740
you are going to get
activation of the legs,

01:20:40.740 --> 01:20:42.480
of course not to the
same degree as you would

01:20:42.480 --> 01:20:44.940
with squats or dead lifts or
leg extensions or leg curls.

01:20:44.940 --> 01:20:46.440
That's simply not the case.

01:20:46.440 --> 01:20:49.738
But you're going to trigger
strength and hypertrophy

01:20:49.738 --> 01:20:52.770
and other types of adaptations
in those muscle groups.

01:20:52.770 --> 01:20:56.250
So this for me also represents
the second leg workout

01:20:56.250 --> 01:20:58.830
of the week where I'm
not touching any weights.

01:20:58.830 --> 01:21:00.480
One important point that
I don't think I've heard

01:21:00.480 --> 01:21:03.690
mentioned anywhere else,
but that I hope to have

01:21:03.690 --> 01:21:05.580
Dr. Kelly Starrett on
the podcast to discuss

01:21:05.580 --> 01:21:08.280
and that I've discussed
with him one on one,

01:21:08.280 --> 01:21:12.120
which is be careful with
all out sprints or all

01:21:12.120 --> 01:21:14.010
out anything cardiovascular exercise,

01:21:14.010 --> 01:21:15.523
you can get injured doing those.

01:21:15.523 --> 01:21:17.910
So for instance, if you
go out and you just sprint

01:21:17.910 --> 01:21:19.710
across a field, all out,

01:21:19.710 --> 01:21:21.870
20 or 30 seconds and
then walk back and can do

01:21:21.870 --> 01:21:23.190
it again and again,

01:21:23.190 --> 01:21:25.410
don't be surprised if the
next day you have some

01:21:25.410 --> 01:21:28.680
sciatica or even some pelvic floor pain.

01:21:28.680 --> 01:21:32.250
I don't recommend going
all out on any movement

01:21:32.250 --> 01:21:35.460
that you can't perform with perfect form.

01:21:35.460 --> 01:21:36.293
Okay?

01:21:36.293 --> 01:21:39.240
So for me, I really try and
stay away from all out sprints.

01:21:39.240 --> 01:21:41.880
I'll sprint it about 95% of what I can do

01:21:41.880 --> 01:21:44.400
because I find if I go all out sprint,

01:21:44.400 --> 01:21:45.450
I don't know what the reason is,

01:21:45.450 --> 01:21:47.430
but it might be an over
extension of a limb

01:21:47.430 --> 01:21:49.110
or something like that,
I'm not a sprinter,

01:21:49.110 --> 01:21:50.220
I'm not a sprinting coach.

01:21:50.220 --> 01:21:54.320
I do hope to get Stu McMillan
on here or Dan Pfaff,

01:21:54.320 --> 01:21:56.130
who are excellent sprinting coaches,

01:21:56.130 --> 01:21:58.920
at some point they were world
class sprinting coaches,

01:21:58.920 --> 01:22:01.770
but I'm not a pro sprinter,

01:22:01.770 --> 01:22:04.857
I'm not even a amateur sprinter,
I'm a fitness sprinter.

01:22:04.857 --> 01:22:08.130
So the Airdyne or assault
bike or the rower is really

01:22:08.130 --> 01:22:09.390
a safer option for me.

01:22:09.390 --> 01:22:11.033
And if I'm running or I'm doing some sort

01:22:11.033 --> 01:22:13.830
of movement where I'm
unconstrained, really,

01:22:13.830 --> 01:22:15.480
in terms of how far my stride is,

01:22:15.480 --> 01:22:17.610
I mean I'm obviously
constrained by the musculature,

01:22:17.610 --> 01:22:20.370
I'm really careful to not overextend or do

01:22:20.370 --> 01:22:21.203
something like that.

01:22:21.203 --> 01:22:22.830
And the only way to do
that is to not go all out.

01:22:22.830 --> 01:22:24.280
So again, the goal for this Friday workout

01:22:24.280 --> 01:22:26.130
is to really get the heart rate high,

01:22:26.130 --> 01:22:28.387
do high intensity interval training...

01:22:28.387 --> 01:22:29.460
A number of different
ways you could do that.

01:22:29.460 --> 01:22:32.520
You can look up HIIT,
HIIT workouts online,

01:22:32.520 --> 01:22:33.990
find the one that's best
for you and really pick

01:22:33.990 --> 01:22:36.450
something that's safe that
you can do consistently,

01:22:36.450 --> 01:22:39.238
and I believe that ideally
will also trigger a bit

01:22:39.238 --> 01:22:42.660
of either strength and
hypertrophy and speed

01:22:42.660 --> 01:22:45.180
power maintenance or even
give you a little bit

01:22:45.180 --> 01:22:47.970
of a stimulus so that by
the time you roll around

01:22:47.970 --> 01:22:50.610
to that leg workout on,
again, on Monday, you've got

01:22:50.610 --> 01:22:53.640
a little bit of an additional
boost to your leg strength,

01:22:53.640 --> 01:22:55.590
hypertrophy, speed and power.

01:22:55.590 --> 01:22:58.414
So we've covered Sunday
through Friday, and then

01:22:58.414 --> 01:23:03.414
Saturday rolls around and
Saturday is when you train arms,

01:23:04.770 --> 01:23:05.820
calves, and neck.

01:23:05.820 --> 01:23:08.520
So this may sound as if
you're training a bunch

01:23:08.520 --> 01:23:11.700
of small muscle groups, biceps,
triceps, necks and calves,

01:23:11.700 --> 01:23:14.280
and that's true, but I
should mention that you are

01:23:14.280 --> 01:23:18.210
also training your torso a
second time and you're doing

01:23:18.210 --> 01:23:21.021
it indirectly, or
sometimes not indirectly.

01:23:21.021 --> 01:23:22.170
Why do I say this?

01:23:22.170 --> 01:23:23.134
Well, keep in mind, again,

01:23:23.134 --> 01:23:24.989
that for strength and hypertrophy,

01:23:24.989 --> 01:23:29.310
you're going for that once
about every 48 to 72 hours,

01:23:29.310 --> 01:23:31.320
you want to stimulate that,

01:23:31.320 --> 01:23:34.110
on Wednesday is when you
train your torso, right?

01:23:34.110 --> 01:23:35.913
Chest, shoulders, back and neck.

01:23:36.960 --> 01:23:39.870
You've had Thursday to
rest, Friday to rest.

01:23:39.870 --> 01:23:41.760
I know a lot of people are
going to want to emphasize

01:23:41.760 --> 01:23:42.750
those body parts and
they're going to think,

01:23:42.750 --> 01:23:44.250
oh, you have to train it twice a week.

01:23:44.250 --> 01:23:48.990
But if you have modest recovery ability

01:23:48.990 --> 01:23:51.660
or low recovery ability, such as I do,

01:23:51.660 --> 01:23:53.340
and you're doing these
other cardiovascular

01:23:53.340 --> 01:23:55.080
training sessions, et cetera,

01:23:55.080 --> 01:23:58.380
well then, on Saturday is
when you will train arms,

01:23:58.380 --> 01:23:59.670
calves and neck directly.

01:23:59.670 --> 01:24:01.200
But included in that,

01:24:01.200 --> 01:24:03.060
remember, two exercises per muscle group,

01:24:03.060 --> 01:24:04.350
one with a peak contraction,

01:24:04.350 --> 01:24:06.600
one with somewhat of a stretch in there.

01:24:06.600 --> 01:24:09.343
Included in that, I
suggest doing some sort

01:24:09.343 --> 01:24:13.350
of dip movement, which I
think it was Pavel Tsatsouline

01:24:13.350 --> 01:24:15.330
said the dip is synonymous

01:24:15.330 --> 01:24:17.700
with or at least similar
to an upper body squat.

01:24:17.700 --> 01:24:19.320
Excuse me, Pavel if I got that wrong.

01:24:19.320 --> 01:24:20.520
Maybe it wasn't you that said that,

01:24:20.520 --> 01:24:22.830
but big admirer of his work,

01:24:22.830 --> 01:24:25.350
and certainly the dip is
a great exercise to hit

01:24:25.350 --> 01:24:29.049
multiple muscle groups,
chest, shoulders, and triceps,

01:24:29.049 --> 01:24:31.140
maybe even some back to
some extent depending

01:24:31.140 --> 01:24:32.190
on how you do it.

01:24:32.190 --> 01:24:36.000
So doing some dipping
movement will indirectly

01:24:36.000 --> 01:24:38.580
stimulate strength hypertrophy, et cetera,

01:24:38.580 --> 01:24:41.910
in the chest and shoulders
and including some sort

01:24:41.910 --> 01:24:43.710
of pulling movement for the bicep,

01:24:43.710 --> 01:24:47.790
like a chin up or palms facing movement,

01:24:47.790 --> 01:24:49.293
pulling up from to the bar,

01:24:50.250 --> 01:24:52.050
especially if it's a
close grip type movement.

01:24:52.050 --> 01:24:54.090
But even if it's a wide
grip type movement,

01:24:54.090 --> 01:24:56.220
will of course trigger strength

01:24:56.220 --> 01:25:00.060
and hypertrophy, maintenance
or improvements in the biceps,

01:25:00.060 --> 01:25:05.060
but will also trigger strength
hypertrophy in the lats

01:25:05.370 --> 01:25:06.330
in the back.

01:25:06.330 --> 01:25:08.490
Okay, so Saturday is this arm workout

01:25:08.490 --> 01:25:10.860
that I'll just give an
example of a potential

01:25:10.860 --> 01:25:13.317
workout where you might
do a few more exercises

01:25:13.317 --> 01:25:14.250
and maybe not just two,

01:25:14.250 --> 01:25:15.480
but maybe three to make sure you get

01:25:15.480 --> 01:25:17.550
the torso indirect stimulation.

01:25:17.550 --> 01:25:18.900
So what would this look like?

01:25:18.900 --> 01:25:21.270
Well, this might be your
sort of classic dumbbell

01:25:21.270 --> 01:25:24.660
curls for the bicep and maybe incline curl

01:25:24.660 --> 01:25:26.220
for the bicep because
it has more of a stretch

01:25:26.220 --> 01:25:27.720
on an incline bench,

01:25:27.720 --> 01:25:31.292
and then you might finish
with two sets of chin ups.

01:25:31.292 --> 01:25:33.000
So palms facing you, chin ups,

01:25:33.000 --> 01:25:34.320
or three sets of chin-ups depending

01:25:34.320 --> 01:25:36.480
on whether or not you're
in a heavier load month

01:25:36.480 --> 01:25:38.730
or a more moderate weight month.

01:25:38.730 --> 01:25:42.450
Again, activating the biceps
muscles 'cause arms day,

01:25:42.450 --> 01:25:45.210
but also activating
strength and hypertrophy

01:25:45.210 --> 01:25:46.740
in the lats or at least maintaining it

01:25:46.740 --> 01:25:49.350
so that, because you're not training

01:25:49.350 --> 01:25:50.670
those torso muscles again

01:25:50.670 --> 01:25:54.180
until Wednesday, you're not
allowing the hypertrophy

01:25:54.180 --> 01:25:56.029
and strength gains that you generated

01:25:56.029 --> 01:25:59.760
on Wednesday to atrophy, to disappear.

01:25:59.760 --> 01:26:02.670
Then, thinking about triceps,

01:26:02.670 --> 01:26:04.620
it might be some sort of triceps isolation

01:26:04.620 --> 01:26:05.850
or peak contraction movement.

01:26:05.850 --> 01:26:07.746
So that could be tricep kickback or some

01:26:07.746 --> 01:26:10.080
overhead extension would
be more of a stretch

01:26:10.080 --> 01:26:11.370
type movement than a kickback.

01:26:11.370 --> 01:26:13.680
But then also doing regular old dips.

01:26:13.680 --> 01:26:15.240
You might even start
with dips, which again,

01:26:15.240 --> 01:26:18.600
are going to activate those
torso muscles and the triceps.

01:26:18.600 --> 01:26:22.530
And then calf work in the same
way that you did on Monday.

01:26:22.530 --> 01:26:23.610
And neck work...

01:26:23.610 --> 01:26:26.010
Again, I am a believer
in training neck multiple

01:26:26.010 --> 01:26:27.003
times per week.

01:26:27.003 --> 01:26:30.970
And if you are able to
finish all of that in 45

01:26:30.970 --> 01:26:32.130
or 50 minutes, great.

01:26:32.130 --> 01:26:33.690
Most people will find
when you're doing a lot

01:26:33.690 --> 01:26:34.523
of small muscle groups,

01:26:34.523 --> 01:26:36.600
it actually takes longer
because you have to go around

01:26:36.600 --> 01:26:37.950
to more exercises.

01:26:37.950 --> 01:26:39.750
But again, just adhere
to the same principles

01:26:39.750 --> 01:26:41.040
we talked about before,

01:26:41.040 --> 01:26:43.800
about 50, five zero, to
60 minutes of real work

01:26:43.800 --> 01:26:46.350
after a warmup with an
asterisk next to that,

01:26:46.350 --> 01:26:48.120
that if someone's on the
equipment or you can't find

01:26:48.120 --> 01:26:50.310
the dumbbells you need, et cetera,

01:26:50.310 --> 01:26:52.290
then maybe 75 minutes max.

01:26:52.290 --> 01:26:55.440
But really trying to not
extend that workout too long,

01:26:55.440 --> 01:26:58.770
making sure that you
activate the arms directly,

01:26:58.770 --> 01:27:01.830
but also activating the
torso muscles indirectly,

01:27:01.830 --> 01:27:04.950
and again, I won't repeat
it this time, again,

01:27:04.950 --> 01:27:07.384
but following the same
weight and repetition

01:27:07.384 --> 01:27:09.733
and rest interval scheme
that we talked about earlier,

01:27:09.733 --> 01:27:11.646
a bit heavier, lower reps,

01:27:11.646 --> 01:27:16.050
more sets and longer
rest for about a month.

01:27:16.050 --> 01:27:19.080
And then alternating to more repetitions

01:27:19.080 --> 01:27:21.813
yet fewer sets, right?

01:27:22.650 --> 01:27:25.560
Shorter rest intervals and
do that for about a month.

01:27:25.560 --> 01:27:27.210
This carries through
for all the resistance

01:27:27.210 --> 01:27:29.670
training workouts regardless
of the day of the week.

01:27:29.670 --> 01:27:31.860
So we've completed the
total arc across the week

01:27:31.860 --> 01:27:34.920
and we can summarize
it as saying Sunday is,

01:27:34.920 --> 01:27:36.360
let's just say long endurance,

01:27:36.360 --> 01:27:38.820
Monday is leg resistance training,

01:27:38.820 --> 01:27:41.070
Tuesday, heat cold contrast,

01:27:41.070 --> 01:27:44.850
Wednesday, torso training plus neck,

01:27:44.850 --> 01:27:48.780
Thursday, I would call
it moderate intensity

01:27:48.780 --> 01:27:51.420
cardiovascular exercise, so that 35 minute

01:27:51.420 --> 01:27:53.280
moderate intensity
cardiovascular exercise,

01:27:53.280 --> 01:27:56.790
Friday, high intensity
interval training of sprinting

01:27:56.790 --> 01:28:00.180
or some variation thereof
and Saturday, arms, calves,

01:28:00.180 --> 01:28:01.983
neck and torso, indirect work.

01:28:02.880 --> 01:28:04.620
That's the total structure.

01:28:04.620 --> 01:28:05.700
But I want to emphasize again,

01:28:05.700 --> 01:28:07.170
you do not need to start this on Sunday.

01:28:07.170 --> 01:28:10.830
That is, you could make the
long endurance work start

01:28:10.830 --> 01:28:13.246
on Tuesday and then just fill in the rest

01:28:13.246 --> 01:28:15.480
as described before.

01:28:15.480 --> 01:28:17.400
It's really up to you.

01:28:17.400 --> 01:28:19.140
There's another important
point I want to make,

01:28:19.140 --> 01:28:23.070
which is that neither
I nor anyone is going

01:28:23.070 --> 01:28:25.870
to be successful in
doing the exact workouts

01:28:25.870 --> 01:28:28.890
on the exact same days of
every week because of travel,

01:28:28.890 --> 01:28:31.680
work, illness, other demands, et cetera.

01:28:31.680 --> 01:28:33.480
The thing about the
schedule that I like so much

01:28:33.480 --> 01:28:36.407
that I do believe that
will benefit you as well

01:28:36.407 --> 01:28:38.520
is that you have some flexibility there.

01:28:38.520 --> 01:28:39.450
What's the flexibility?

01:28:39.450 --> 01:28:42.330
Well, let's say you train
your typical Sunday workout

01:28:42.330 --> 01:28:45.840
of endurance, then you train
legs on Monday and then

01:28:45.840 --> 01:28:49.560
you don't manage to do
your heat cold contrast

01:28:49.560 --> 01:28:51.120
on Tuesday for whatever reason.

01:28:51.120 --> 01:28:53.940
Well, you can put it on Wednesday.

01:28:53.940 --> 01:28:55.380
Just make sure that if you're going to do

01:28:55.380 --> 01:28:58.740
the cold stimulus, that
you don't do it too close,

01:28:58.740 --> 01:29:03.740
not within four, ideally
eight hours after the training

01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:07.230
of torso, but you could do
it before or you could do it

01:29:07.230 --> 01:29:11.130
just heat and skip the cold
that particular week, right?

01:29:11.130 --> 01:29:13.477
Not ideal, but better
than not doing anything.

01:29:13.477 --> 01:29:14.640
Let's say, for instance,

01:29:14.640 --> 01:29:16.200
the leg workout was particularly brutal,

01:29:16.200 --> 01:29:19.410
you don't sleep that well on
Monday night or Tuesday night.

01:29:19.410 --> 01:29:23.130
Well then should you do the
torso workout on Wednesday?

01:29:23.130 --> 01:29:25.020
Well, I would say,

01:29:25.020 --> 01:29:27.150
why not move the heat cold contrast

01:29:27.150 --> 01:29:30.930
to Wednesday and then
push that torso workout

01:29:30.930 --> 01:29:34.890
to Thursday and maybe also
try and do that 35 minute

01:29:34.890 --> 01:29:38.670
run on Thursday every once
in a while rather than lose

01:29:38.670 --> 01:29:40.440
the total control of the program and let

01:29:40.440 --> 01:29:41.670
everything shuffle forward.

01:29:41.670 --> 01:29:43.470
Here's the basic principle.

01:29:43.470 --> 01:29:46.530
I do believe that any
one of these workouts,

01:29:46.530 --> 01:29:48.270
whether it's for endurance
or resistance training,

01:29:48.270 --> 01:29:50.560
can be shifted either
one day forward or one

01:29:50.560 --> 01:29:52.890
day back, right?

01:29:52.890 --> 01:29:55.104
You could delay it by a
day or you could accelerate

01:29:55.104 --> 01:29:58.530
it by a day in order to
make sure that you get

01:29:58.530 --> 01:30:00.930
everything done across the week.

01:30:00.930 --> 01:30:02.970
In fact, I would say the best way to think

01:30:02.970 --> 01:30:06.342
about this foundational
fitness program is not

01:30:06.342 --> 01:30:09.930
from the details up,
but from the top down,

01:30:09.930 --> 01:30:12.150
from the big picture down to the details,

01:30:12.150 --> 01:30:13.290
and say to yourself,

01:30:13.290 --> 01:30:16.350
once a week you're going to
get some long endurance in,

01:30:16.350 --> 01:30:17.430
another day during the week,

01:30:17.430 --> 01:30:18.720
you're going to make
sure that you get a kind

01:30:18.720 --> 01:30:21.780
of moderate faster endurance workout in,

01:30:21.780 --> 01:30:23.940
and then one other day during the week,

01:30:23.940 --> 01:30:25.560
you're going to get an all out sprint,

01:30:25.560 --> 01:30:29.100
high intensity cardiovascular
exercise workout in.

01:30:29.100 --> 01:30:31.950
You're going to get those
three workouts in somehow.

01:30:31.950 --> 01:30:32.970
And then in addition to that,

01:30:32.970 --> 01:30:35.340
you will also do resistance training

01:30:35.340 --> 01:30:37.470
for every muscle group in your body.

01:30:37.470 --> 01:30:41.430
And that means doing your legs
hard at least once a week,

01:30:41.430 --> 01:30:45.810
your torso hard at least once
a week and your arms hard

01:30:45.810 --> 01:30:46.643
at least once a week.

01:30:46.643 --> 01:30:48.390
And of course you are
also paying attention

01:30:48.390 --> 01:30:49.610
to training your calves.

01:30:49.610 --> 01:30:52.260
And I do, for reasons I described before,

01:30:52.260 --> 01:30:54.480
believe that you want to
train your neck at least

01:30:54.480 --> 01:30:55.410
to keep it strong.

01:30:55.410 --> 01:30:58.140
You may not want to
generate hypertrophy there.

01:30:58.140 --> 01:31:00.420
People vary in terms of how
quickly their neck grows.

01:31:00.420 --> 01:31:01.650
Some people grows very, very fast.

01:31:01.650 --> 01:31:03.000
Other people, for the life of them,

01:31:03.000 --> 01:31:04.560
they can't get much
hypertrophy in their neck.

01:31:04.560 --> 01:31:06.120
But keeping that neck strong,

01:31:06.120 --> 01:31:09.060
at least through some very
light work to moderate

01:31:09.060 --> 01:31:10.953
weight work, very, very important,

01:31:11.910 --> 01:31:13.590
for reasons I stated earlier.

01:31:13.590 --> 01:31:16.440
If you set out those goals,

01:31:16.440 --> 01:31:19.849
then the specific days that
you do each workout isn't

01:31:19.849 --> 01:31:22.830
as critical, but the specific spacing is.

01:31:22.830 --> 01:31:24.570
So for instance, you're
not going to want to do

01:31:24.570 --> 01:31:26.910
your high intensity
interval training the day

01:31:26.910 --> 01:31:28.590
after you train your legs,

01:31:28.590 --> 01:31:29.423
because if you're doing

01:31:29.423 --> 01:31:31.560
that high intensity
interval training correctly,

01:31:31.560 --> 01:31:33.210
you're going to be

01:31:33.210 --> 01:31:35.970
taxing your legs and
eating into their recovery.

01:31:35.970 --> 01:31:38.310
And so you want to space them
out by two or three days.

01:31:38.310 --> 01:31:39.845
So I think you'll notice
that the point is really

01:31:39.845 --> 01:31:44.400
to optimize everything on
the whole rather than any

01:31:44.400 --> 01:31:47.880
one specific aspect of
training or adaptation.

01:31:47.880 --> 01:31:51.120
Now that said, I do realize
that some people might

01:31:51.120 --> 01:31:53.460
be hyper focused on things like strength

01:31:53.460 --> 01:31:55.993
and hypertrophy and the
aesthetics that come with it.

01:31:55.993 --> 01:31:57.870
A key point about strength hypertrophy

01:31:57.870 --> 01:31:59.640
and weight training, and this is something

01:31:59.640 --> 01:32:01.590
that has been covered
on multiple podcasts,

01:32:01.590 --> 01:32:02.910
certainly the one with Jeff Cavaliere

01:32:02.910 --> 01:32:04.860
and with Dr. Andy Galpin and the one

01:32:04.860 --> 01:32:08.880
that I did on building muscle
strength and hypertrophy,

01:32:08.880 --> 01:32:09.933
the solo episode.

01:32:11.070 --> 01:32:12.873
And that is the following,

01:32:14.010 --> 01:32:16.770
it is the rare individual
who has perfectly

01:32:16.770 --> 01:32:18.750
balanced musculature, right?

01:32:18.750 --> 01:32:21.600
Most people can be a bit quad dominant

01:32:21.600 --> 01:32:23.550
or hamstring dominant,
or they have trouble

01:32:23.550 --> 01:32:26.580
activating their glutes
or somebody has a terrible

01:32:26.580 --> 01:32:28.860
time trying to activate
their chest muscles,

01:32:28.860 --> 01:32:31.530
but they're very strong
in the back, et cetera.

01:32:31.530 --> 01:32:35.700
It's very clear that we can
know that not just based

01:32:35.700 --> 01:32:38.640
on aesthetics, right, but based

01:32:38.640 --> 01:32:41.940
on deliberate contractibility
of those muscles.

01:32:41.940 --> 01:32:43.650
So I don't want to get into
this in too much detail

01:32:43.650 --> 01:32:44.610
for sake of time,

01:32:44.610 --> 01:32:47.220
but this is something that
has peer reviewed research

01:32:47.220 --> 01:32:49.710
to support it and was
also discussed extensively

01:32:49.710 --> 01:32:52.050
with Jeff Cavaliere when he was a guest.

01:32:52.050 --> 01:32:54.570
And that actually he's really
popularized this notion

01:32:54.570 --> 01:32:55.710
and it's absolutely true,

01:32:55.710 --> 01:32:58.350
which is that if you can
contract a muscle very hard

01:32:58.350 --> 01:33:00.060
to the point where it almost
feels like it's cramping,

01:33:00.060 --> 01:33:02.070
if you can do that even
when there's no weight

01:33:02.070 --> 01:33:04.860
in your hand or there's
no resistance against it,

01:33:04.860 --> 01:33:07.050
so you're just using your
mind muscle connection

01:33:07.050 --> 01:33:08.820
to contract that muscle
hard and isolate it,

01:33:08.820 --> 01:33:11.640
chances are you'll be able
to generate hypertrophy

01:33:11.640 --> 01:33:14.280
and strength gains pretty
easily in that muscle

01:33:14.280 --> 01:33:17.910
compared to muscles that you
have a harder time activating.

01:33:17.910 --> 01:33:20.430
So during all resistance
training, that mind muscle link

01:33:20.430 --> 01:33:24.060
is really important, so much
so that some people will

01:33:24.060 --> 01:33:26.280
even try and emphasize
contraction of the muscles

01:33:26.280 --> 01:33:27.626
in between sets, et cetera.

01:33:27.626 --> 01:33:30.630
I personally, because I'm
not somebody who likes

01:33:30.630 --> 01:33:33.840
a mirror when I work out, and
I'm not somebody who wants

01:33:33.840 --> 01:33:35.847
to spend time in between
sets flexing muscles

01:33:35.847 --> 01:33:38.340
and et cetera for whatever reason,

01:33:38.340 --> 01:33:39.780
I want to actually rest between sets,

01:33:39.780 --> 01:33:41.760
and I'm more concerned with performance

01:33:41.760 --> 01:33:43.260
during those sets and
really putting my mind

01:33:43.260 --> 01:33:45.270
into the muscle during the set,

01:33:45.270 --> 01:33:48.150
I really try and emphasize
deep relaxation between sets.

01:33:48.150 --> 01:33:50.490
And so here's a tool that again is built

01:33:50.490 --> 01:33:53.550
out of science and I should
say peer reviewed studies,

01:33:53.550 --> 01:33:55.110
some of which are being done in my lab,

01:33:55.110 --> 01:33:56.310
but other labs as well,

01:33:56.310 --> 01:33:59.010
which is that in between
sets what I really strive

01:33:59.010 --> 01:34:01.920
to do is to bring my heart
rate down as much as possible,

01:34:01.920 --> 01:34:03.810
calm myself down as much as possible,

01:34:03.810 --> 01:34:06.420
and I'll do the so-called
physiological sigh in order

01:34:06.420 --> 01:34:07.253
to do that.

01:34:07.253 --> 01:34:11.073
That's two inhales through the
nose, back to back, [sighs]

01:34:11.940 --> 01:34:14.370
and then long full
exhale through the mouth.

01:34:14.370 --> 01:34:17.130
I just did it partially there
for the sake of time, again.

01:34:17.130 --> 01:34:19.830
So a big deep inhale through
the nose and then sneak

01:34:19.830 --> 01:34:21.300
in a little bit more on a second inhale

01:34:21.300 --> 01:34:22.880
to maximally inflate the
lungs and the alveoli

01:34:22.880 --> 01:34:26.790
in the lungs, and then a
full exhale of all your air

01:34:26.790 --> 01:34:28.380
via the mouth to empty your lungs.

01:34:28.380 --> 01:34:32.206
That's the fastest way that
we are aware of to calm

01:34:32.206 --> 01:34:34.020
your nervous system down.

01:34:34.020 --> 01:34:36.210
And really, in between sets you can use

01:34:36.210 --> 01:34:38.340
that to calm yourself
down and conserve energy.

01:34:38.340 --> 01:34:42.180
But then as you move into
the weight training set,

01:34:42.180 --> 01:34:45.180
you really want to ratchet
up your focus and attention

01:34:45.180 --> 01:34:46.740
to the muscles that
you're going to be using.

01:34:46.740 --> 01:34:48.600
Now, I'd like to acknowledge
that there's a huge range

01:34:48.600 --> 01:34:51.270
of parameters in terms of
how to actually perform

01:34:51.270 --> 01:34:52.103
during the set.

01:34:52.103 --> 01:34:53.958
You can focus on a
particular muscle and try

01:34:53.958 --> 01:34:56.760
and really isolate from the
beginning of the movement.

01:34:56.760 --> 01:34:58.650
Some people will really
try and isolate it only

01:34:58.650 --> 01:34:59.640
during the peak contraction.

01:34:59.640 --> 01:35:01.050
Some people accentuate the negative.

01:35:01.050 --> 01:35:02.123
There's speed and cadence.

01:35:02.123 --> 01:35:05.910
There are, again,
remember, concepts are few,

01:35:05.910 --> 01:35:06.750
methods are many.

01:35:06.750 --> 01:35:08.460
And if you're interested
in the various methods

01:35:08.460 --> 01:35:11.520
of eccentrics and concentrics
and all the different

01:35:11.520 --> 01:35:15.030
ways of changing up cadence
and so forth during sets,

01:35:15.030 --> 01:35:17.130
there's an enormous amount
of quality information

01:35:17.130 --> 01:35:20.190
out there, far too much for
us to get into detail now.

01:35:20.190 --> 01:35:21.750
But what I describe the general principles

01:35:21.750 --> 01:35:24.960
of how to set your mind, if
you will, during the set,

01:35:24.960 --> 01:35:26.820
you should be focused on the
muscles that you're using

01:35:26.820 --> 01:35:27.870
and or moving the weight.

01:35:27.870 --> 01:35:29.430
If movement of the
weight is more important,

01:35:29.430 --> 01:35:31.290
you can either focus on moving the weight

01:35:31.290 --> 01:35:33.600
or challenging muscles, right?

01:35:33.600 --> 01:35:35.310
You can either try and
isolate muscles and make

01:35:35.310 --> 01:35:38.760
specific muscles do the work
or simply moving the weight.

01:35:38.760 --> 01:35:40.920
Moving the weight is
going to be more geared

01:35:40.920 --> 01:35:44.880
towards strength
improvements, but focusing

01:35:44.880 --> 01:35:46.770
on the muscle, so called
mind muscle link is going to

01:35:46.770 --> 01:35:49.620
shift that very same set
more toward hypertrophy.

01:35:49.620 --> 01:35:51.480
I realize I'm painting
with a broad brush here,

01:35:51.480 --> 01:35:53.670
but nonetheless this
is grounded in the way

01:35:53.670 --> 01:35:56.910
that the nervous system
governs muscular contraction.

01:35:56.910 --> 01:35:58.449
And while I think most people are familiar

01:35:58.449 --> 01:36:00.750
with the number of different
variables associated

01:36:00.750 --> 01:36:03.810
with the resistance training,
sets, reps, rest intervals,

01:36:03.810 --> 01:36:07.335
cadence, et cetera, there
are also a tremendous number

01:36:07.335 --> 01:36:10.230
of very important variables
for endurance in any kind

01:36:10.230 --> 01:36:11.940
of cardiovascular training.

01:36:11.940 --> 01:36:13.740
And there are a lot of excellent resources

01:36:13.740 --> 01:36:15.630
out there about that.

01:36:15.630 --> 01:36:17.970
I think the most important one,

01:36:17.970 --> 01:36:20.400
in fact I will go on record
saying what I believe

01:36:20.400 --> 01:36:24.690
to be the most important
variable for any endurance

01:36:24.690 --> 01:36:25.770
or cardiovascular training

01:36:25.770 --> 01:36:28.560
is that because it's
a repetitive movement,

01:36:28.560 --> 01:36:31.500
that you are able to
complete the movement safely,

01:36:31.500 --> 01:36:34.020
meaning you're not putting
your body into range

01:36:34.020 --> 01:36:37.830
of motion or into positions
that can damage joints

01:36:37.830 --> 01:36:40.440
or put you in any kind
of compromised state.

01:36:40.440 --> 01:36:41.273
And some people might think, well,

01:36:41.273 --> 01:36:42.420
that seems kind of silly.

01:36:42.420 --> 01:36:44.250
But if you've ever set the, for instance,

01:36:44.250 --> 01:36:46.140
the seat too high on a
stationary bike and then

01:36:46.140 --> 01:36:50.250
done Airdyne or assault bike
type interval training sprints,

01:36:50.250 --> 01:36:53.700
if it's set too high and you're
over-striding, as it were,

01:36:53.700 --> 01:36:56.160
the next day, you can really
pay the price in terms

01:36:56.160 --> 01:36:57.690
of some back pain or sciatica.

01:36:57.690 --> 01:37:00.300
And sometimes that pain can
extend for quite a while.

01:37:00.300 --> 01:37:02.190
So of course you don't want
to approach any exercise

01:37:02.190 --> 01:37:04.198
with so much caution that
it's neurotic and preventive

01:37:04.198 --> 01:37:07.530
and yet you don't want to
approach any exercise in any way

01:37:07.530 --> 01:37:10.290
that's so cavalier,

01:37:10.290 --> 01:37:13.350
forgive the pun, Jeff,
that you're also going to

01:37:13.350 --> 01:37:15.540
compromise the integrity of your joints

01:37:15.540 --> 01:37:17.280
and musculature and connective tissue.

01:37:17.280 --> 01:37:20.640
Let's talk about some real
world practical variables.

01:37:20.640 --> 01:37:21.900
For instance,

01:37:21.900 --> 01:37:25.560
let's say you get a poor
to terrible night's sleep.

01:37:25.560 --> 01:37:27.720
Should you train the next day or not?

01:37:27.720 --> 01:37:29.160
Well, that really depends.

01:37:29.160 --> 01:37:31.350
I can honestly say I've
had some of the best

01:37:31.350 --> 01:37:33.000
training sessions, resistance training

01:37:33.000 --> 01:37:34.890
or endurance training
sessions after a really

01:37:34.890 --> 01:37:36.270
poor night's sleep.

01:37:36.270 --> 01:37:38.310
But that's the rare event.

01:37:38.310 --> 01:37:42.150
More often than not, if
I'm not sleeping well,

01:37:42.150 --> 01:37:43.350
I've had a terrible night's sleep,

01:37:43.350 --> 01:37:46.920
the next day I will just
skip training that day.

01:37:46.920 --> 01:37:48.870
I know that will shock a
number of you out there,

01:37:48.870 --> 01:37:50.880
or perhaps you're
already calling me names,

01:37:50.880 --> 01:37:52.230
weak, et cetera.

01:37:52.230 --> 01:37:55.110
But I find that if I've slept
really poorly or I've had

01:37:55.110 --> 01:37:57.958
a very stressful event
the day before and I don't

01:37:57.958 --> 01:38:00.750
sleep well, training
the next day sets me up

01:38:00.750 --> 01:38:02.760
for getting ill and getting
ill sets me up for not

01:38:02.760 --> 01:38:04.980
being able to train for multiple days.

01:38:04.980 --> 01:38:08.400
So it is my preference in
that case to skip a day

01:38:08.400 --> 01:38:10.432
and really focus on recovery.

01:38:10.432 --> 01:38:12.810
And then, as I mentioned earlier,

01:38:12.810 --> 01:38:17.580
slide that workout to the
next day and rarely double

01:38:17.580 --> 01:38:19.260
that workout up with another workout,

01:38:19.260 --> 01:38:21.690
but then just slide the
schedule forward by a day.

01:38:21.690 --> 01:38:23.460
But I really try and strive,

01:38:23.460 --> 01:38:26.730
that is, I really try to
double up at least some

01:38:26.730 --> 01:38:28.830
workouts later in the week in that case,

01:38:28.830 --> 01:38:32.100
so that I can get back
on schedule of starting

01:38:32.100 --> 01:38:34.860
the seven day protocol
again on the same day.

01:38:34.860 --> 01:38:36.750
I don't want to be
excessively vague there.

01:38:36.750 --> 01:38:38.370
What I'm trying to say is I try and adhere

01:38:38.370 --> 01:38:40.500
to the same schedule, but if
I get a poor night's sleep,

01:38:40.500 --> 01:38:43.410
I'll just simply skip
the workout the next day,

01:38:43.410 --> 01:38:44.880
slide the workout forward.

01:38:44.880 --> 01:38:46.140
There is one exception to that,

01:38:46.140 --> 01:38:47.400
and it's an important exception,

01:38:47.400 --> 01:38:50.880
which is there are times
when I've not slept well

01:38:50.880 --> 01:38:53.370
or I've had some
particularly stressful event

01:38:53.370 --> 01:38:55.533
the day before and haven't slept well,

01:38:56.580 --> 01:38:58.530
but I'm able to do so-called NSDR,

01:38:58.530 --> 01:39:00.300
non-sleep deep rest the next day.

01:39:00.300 --> 01:39:02.730
So there have been times
when I've only got three

01:39:02.730 --> 01:39:04.080
or four hours of sleep the night

01:39:04.080 --> 01:39:06.420
before and I'm feeling
really behind the ball

01:39:06.420 --> 01:39:08.880
the next morning, but I really
want to get my workout in.

01:39:08.880 --> 01:39:11.940
So instead what I will
do is a 10, but ideally

01:39:11.940 --> 01:39:15.630
in that case a 30 or even 60
minute non-sleep deep rest.

01:39:15.630 --> 01:39:16.830
And there's a 10 minute

01:39:16.830 --> 01:39:20.879
non-sleep deep breath protocol read by me.

01:39:20.879 --> 01:39:24.930
But it is a non-spiritual, non-mystical,

01:39:24.930 --> 01:39:27.960
science-supported non-sleep
deep breath protocol

01:39:27.960 --> 01:39:28.793
available on YouTube.

01:39:28.793 --> 01:39:31.517
You can simply put my
name, Huberman, put NSDR,

01:39:31.517 --> 01:39:34.653
and Virtusan, V-I-R-T-U-S-A-N,

01:39:35.580 --> 01:39:37.230
into YouTube and you'll find that script.

01:39:37.230 --> 01:39:39.660
There are other NSDR scripts
that you can find now

01:39:39.660 --> 01:39:41.640
on Spotify and on YouTube.

01:39:41.640 --> 01:39:42.630
And if you fall asleep

01:39:42.630 --> 01:39:45.210
during those non-sleep deep
rest scripts, that's great.

01:39:45.210 --> 01:39:46.050
And if you don't,

01:39:46.050 --> 01:39:48.540
you will also find that it
will restore your ability

01:39:48.540 --> 01:39:50.730
to perform mental and physical work.

01:39:50.730 --> 01:39:53.757
So there are times when I
haven't gotten as much sleep

01:39:53.757 --> 01:39:54.660
as I would like,

01:39:54.660 --> 01:39:57.480
or I'm feeling a bit more
stressed for whatever reason,

01:39:57.480 --> 01:40:00.840
and I'll do NSDR, and
then I will go train.

01:40:00.840 --> 01:40:03.330
And that often works
fabulously well for me.

01:40:03.330 --> 01:40:05.430
And then I don't have to
skip a workout entirely

01:40:05.430 --> 01:40:07.110
just because I didn't
get a good night's sleep.

01:40:07.110 --> 01:40:09.810
A lot of people ask whether
or not you should train

01:40:09.810 --> 01:40:12.570
fasted or fed, and this is
a very controversial area.

01:40:12.570 --> 01:40:15.047
I personally prefer to
do my cardiovascular work

01:40:15.047 --> 01:40:17.320
not having eaten anything

01:40:18.180 --> 01:40:21.210
in the previous 3 to 10 hours.

01:40:21.210 --> 01:40:23.640
And typically that's because
I wake up and I'll do

01:40:23.640 --> 01:40:26.190
the cardiovascular training
within about an hour

01:40:26.190 --> 01:40:28.230
of waking up, sometimes later,

01:40:28.230 --> 01:40:30.510
because my first meal
generally falls, generally,

01:40:30.510 --> 01:40:32.490
not always, falls around 11:00 AM.

01:40:32.490 --> 01:40:35.010
I don't do any kind of
formal intermittent fasting,

01:40:35.010 --> 01:40:38.820
but typically my meal schedule
somewhere between 11:00 a.m.

01:40:38.820 --> 01:40:40.980
and my last bite of
food is around 8:00 p.m.

01:40:40.980 --> 01:40:42.450
but I'm not super strict about that.

01:40:42.450 --> 01:40:45.496
I might eat in as late
as 9:00 p.m. and I might

01:40:45.496 --> 01:40:48.000
eat something at 10:00 a.m.
if I wake up really hungry,

01:40:48.000 --> 01:40:49.800
I might have something
before 11:00 a.m., I'm not

01:40:49.800 --> 01:40:51.120
neurotic about it.

01:40:51.120 --> 01:40:52.500
But in terms of training,

01:40:52.500 --> 01:40:54.960
I like to train fasted and that includes

01:40:54.960 --> 01:40:56.970
the resistance training
workouts and those come early

01:40:56.970 --> 01:40:57.990
in the day for me.

01:40:57.990 --> 01:40:59.730
And typically if I'm going to train legs

01:40:59.730 --> 01:41:02.303
on Monday, for instance,
which is when I train legs,

01:41:02.303 --> 01:41:04.860
I'll make sure that the
night before I'm ingesting

01:41:04.860 --> 01:41:06.930
some starch, some carbohydrate,

01:41:06.930 --> 01:41:10.013
like rice or pasta or
something of that sort to make

01:41:10.013 --> 01:41:12.330
sure that when I do that
morning leg workout,

01:41:12.330 --> 01:41:14.430
I have enough glycogen in
the muscles, et cetera.

01:41:14.430 --> 01:41:16.710
Again, nutrition is a
somewhat controversial area.

01:41:16.710 --> 01:41:19.890
In fact, it can evoke very
strong feelings 'cause

01:41:19.890 --> 01:41:21.474
I know we've got vegans
and we've got omnivores

01:41:21.474 --> 01:41:24.150
and we've got carnivores
and people who are keto.

01:41:24.150 --> 01:41:26.010
This isn't really the format for us to get

01:41:26.010 --> 01:41:26.883
into all of that.

01:41:27.720 --> 01:41:29.910
I think the rule to follow is figure

01:41:29.910 --> 01:41:31.860
out what optimizes your training

01:41:31.860 --> 01:41:33.870
for your particular training goals.

01:41:33.870 --> 01:41:36.780
For me, that most often
means training fasted

01:41:36.780 --> 01:41:38.760
and then eating pretty soon after I train.

01:41:38.760 --> 01:41:42.622
And if it's a high intensity
resistance training workout,

01:41:42.622 --> 01:41:44.592
and frankly, all of my
resistance training workouts

01:41:44.592 --> 01:41:45.900
are pretty high intensity,

01:41:45.900 --> 01:41:48.510
I'm not going to failure on
every set, but at least, say,

01:41:48.510 --> 01:41:51.420
about 30% of those sets
I'm going to failure.

01:41:51.420 --> 01:41:54.210
And the other sets I'm
working very hard nonetheless,

01:41:54.210 --> 01:41:55.980
well then I eat some
starches after I train

01:41:55.980 --> 01:41:57.600
and I also ingest some protein in the form

01:41:57.600 --> 01:41:59.730
of a protein drink or a meal that includes

01:41:59.730 --> 01:42:01.200
some protein food.

01:42:01.200 --> 01:42:04.470
But I don't like to eat before
I do resistance training

01:42:04.470 --> 01:42:06.150
or at least not within the hour or two

01:42:06.150 --> 01:42:08.250
before I do resistance training.

01:42:08.250 --> 01:42:09.147
There are exceptions to that,

01:42:09.147 --> 01:42:11.490
and I should say that the
same basically applies

01:42:11.490 --> 01:42:12.540
to endurance work.

01:42:12.540 --> 01:42:14.280
If I'm going to head out for a run,

01:42:14.280 --> 01:42:17.280
typically I don't want my
belly full of food or any

01:42:17.280 --> 01:42:21.000
food at all, but there
are times where I wake up

01:42:21.000 --> 01:42:23.250
hungry and I very much
need to eat something

01:42:23.250 --> 01:42:25.260
or I have something
scheduled socially like

01:42:25.260 --> 01:42:28.140
a breakfast and I'll have
that breakfast and then

01:42:28.140 --> 01:42:30.356
an hour or 90 minutes
later I'll do my workout

01:42:30.356 --> 01:42:33.480
because I want to make sure
that I finish the workout.

01:42:33.480 --> 01:42:35.656
I, again, am not neurotically
attached to training

01:42:35.656 --> 01:42:37.380
fasted or fed.

01:42:37.380 --> 01:42:40.320
For me, fasted is preferred,
but if I have to train fed,

01:42:40.320 --> 01:42:42.450
better to train than to not train at all.

01:42:42.450 --> 01:42:44.550
We haven't talked so much
about flexibility yet,

01:42:44.550 --> 01:42:47.190
but we did an entire episode
of the Huberman Lab podcast

01:42:47.190 --> 01:42:50.100
on flexibility and I
encourage you to check

01:42:50.100 --> 01:42:52.470
out that episode if you're interested

01:42:52.470 --> 01:42:53.984
in increasing your flexibility.

01:42:53.984 --> 01:42:56.820
But the basic takeaway from that episode

01:42:56.820 --> 01:42:58.950
is that if you look at what
I like to call the center

01:42:58.950 --> 01:43:01.200
of mass of the research,

01:43:01.200 --> 01:43:03.450
that is most of the studies
and what the conclusions

01:43:03.450 --> 01:43:05.220
of most of the quality studies point to,

01:43:05.220 --> 01:43:06.210
so not the exceptions,

01:43:06.210 --> 01:43:09.120
but the kind of general
rules that have been gleaned

01:43:09.120 --> 01:43:11.340
over time from multiple labs over multiple

01:43:11.340 --> 01:43:12.393
decades, et cetera.

01:43:12.393 --> 01:43:15.180
What you find is that static stretching,

01:43:15.180 --> 01:43:18.690
that is, holding a stretch
and in fact exhaling

01:43:18.690 --> 01:43:23.370
and relaxing the midsection
and torso and relaxing

01:43:23.370 --> 01:43:25.496
into the stretch as opposed
to staying full of air

01:43:25.496 --> 01:43:29.040
and tense, but mentally
and physically relaxing

01:43:29.040 --> 01:43:33.030
into the stretch, but
not stretching maximally,

01:43:33.030 --> 01:43:36.090
that is not extending as
far as you possibly can go,

01:43:36.090 --> 01:43:38.550
but more like 60% or even less.

01:43:38.550 --> 01:43:42.240
And then holding those
static stretches for anywhere

01:43:42.240 --> 01:43:45.150
from 30 to 60 seconds and then repeating,

01:43:45.150 --> 01:43:47.220
doing that two or three
times throughout the week

01:43:47.220 --> 01:43:48.210
for multiple muscle groups,

01:43:48.210 --> 01:43:49.590
so it could be for your quadriceps,

01:43:49.590 --> 01:43:51.780
could be for hamstrings, for your lats.

01:43:51.780 --> 01:43:52.770
There are protocols out there.

01:43:52.770 --> 01:43:55.913
In fact, we have a newsletter
that is focused entirely

01:43:55.913 --> 01:43:58.200
on protocols for
flexibility and stretching.

01:43:58.200 --> 01:44:00.690
You can find that again
by going hubermanlab.com.

01:44:00.690 --> 01:44:02.340
You don't even need to
sign up for the newsletter,

01:44:02.340 --> 01:44:04.200
although we invite you to if you like,

01:44:04.200 --> 01:44:05.331
but you can simply go there,

01:44:05.331 --> 01:44:08.520
scroll down to the
flexibility newsletter and all

01:44:08.520 --> 01:44:10.620
the protocols are there
for each of the muscle

01:44:10.620 --> 01:44:11.790
groups, et cetera.

01:44:11.790 --> 01:44:14.040
But what I typically try
and do is some stretching

01:44:14.040 --> 01:44:16.520
in the evening, because
I train in the morning,

01:44:16.520 --> 01:44:21.520
as I'm perhaps getting ready
for bed or if the TV is on,

01:44:21.930 --> 01:44:24.360
which in our house doesn't typically go

01:44:24.360 --> 01:44:26.340
on because we don't
have a TV, but of course

01:44:26.340 --> 01:44:28.110
there are computers and people are

01:44:28.110 --> 01:44:29.422
on their computers, et cetera.

01:44:29.422 --> 01:44:32.010
Well, I'll try and do some
stretching while I do that.

01:44:32.010 --> 01:44:34.740
I also have a standing desk,
so during the day at work,

01:44:34.740 --> 01:44:37.170
regardless of whether or not
I train that morning or not,

01:44:37.170 --> 01:44:38.340
or I'm going to train in the afternoon,

01:44:38.340 --> 01:44:40.830
I'll try and do some static
stretching for my hamstrings,

01:44:40.830 --> 01:44:43.484
my quads, my lats, my shoulders, my back,

01:44:43.484 --> 01:44:45.570
really doesn't take much
time and I really try

01:44:45.570 --> 01:44:48.480
to space that out
throughout the week, which,

01:44:48.480 --> 01:44:50.340
if you look at the peer reviewed research,

01:44:50.340 --> 01:44:52.590
matches well to what's
known to be most effective,

01:44:52.590 --> 01:44:53.670
which are going to be short,

01:44:53.670 --> 01:44:55.890
repeated sessions ideally every day.

01:44:55.890 --> 01:45:00.210
But truth told, I fail.
I categorically fail.

01:45:00.210 --> 01:45:01.800
I was about to think of
whether or not I ever

01:45:01.800 --> 01:45:02.790
stretch every day.

01:45:02.790 --> 01:45:03.840
I fail to do it every day,

01:45:03.840 --> 01:45:06.667
but I get about three or
so stretching sessions

01:45:06.667 --> 01:45:07.680
in per week.

01:45:07.680 --> 01:45:09.120
And again, it's just static hold,

01:45:09.120 --> 01:45:11.610
trying to really relax into the stretch.

01:45:11.610 --> 01:45:14.220
Now the relax into the
stretch is something

01:45:14.220 --> 01:45:17.220
has been talked about
in martial arts circles

01:45:17.220 --> 01:45:19.680
and Pavel Tsatsouline has an
excellent book on stretching,

01:45:19.680 --> 01:45:22.470
we can provide a link to
that, talks about this,

01:45:22.470 --> 01:45:25.350
has a lot to do with relaxation
of the nervous system

01:45:25.350 --> 01:45:27.000
and the way that the
nerves innervate muscles

01:45:27.000 --> 01:45:29.010
and allow for stretch, if you will.

01:45:29.010 --> 01:45:30.600
Also, the way that the
tendons and ligaments

01:45:30.600 --> 01:45:33.180
are innervated by nerves.

01:45:33.180 --> 01:45:34.620
The converse is also true.

01:45:34.620 --> 01:45:36.733
And here, again, this is
a principle that Pavel has

01:45:36.733 --> 01:45:39.930
put forth, I believe he
calls it irradiation,

01:45:39.930 --> 01:45:43.560
meaning irradiating out or
emanating out from a source,

01:45:43.560 --> 01:45:47.192
which is that while exhaling
and relaxing the torso,

01:45:47.192 --> 01:45:50.133
the midsection, some
people call it the core,

01:45:51.090 --> 01:45:53.550
although some people don't like that term,

01:45:53.550 --> 01:45:56.790
can facilitate relaxation and stretching

01:45:56.790 --> 01:45:58.530
through a larger range of motion.

01:45:58.530 --> 01:46:03.000
So too can contracting
the core, the midsection,

01:46:03.000 --> 01:46:06.150
or gripping very tightly with the fist can

01:46:06.150 --> 01:46:08.820
facilitate muscular
contraction because of the way

01:46:08.820 --> 01:46:11.190
that the nervous system heavily,

01:46:11.190 --> 01:46:15.450
we can even say over-represents
the fists in the brain.

01:46:15.450 --> 01:46:16.710
And so how would you apply

01:46:16.710 --> 01:46:19.347
this to your overall
foundational fitness protocol?

01:46:19.347 --> 01:46:21.150
Well, it turns out that, let's say,

01:46:21.150 --> 01:46:25.590
you're doing a movement that
involves one limb moving

01:46:25.590 --> 01:46:27.510
and then the other, let's
say it's bicep curls,

01:46:27.510 --> 01:46:28.950
just for sake of example,

01:46:28.950 --> 01:46:31.470
turns out that you will
actually be stronger

01:46:31.470 --> 01:46:34.443
in moving that dumbbell
with the arm that happens

01:46:34.443 --> 01:46:38.760
to be moving if you grip
the handle very tightly,

01:46:38.760 --> 01:46:41.370
but also grip the handle of the opposite

01:46:41.370 --> 01:46:42.960
dumbbell very tightly.

01:46:42.960 --> 01:46:44.670
Now that said, in between sets,

01:46:44.670 --> 01:46:46.320
I encourage you to do the opposite.

01:46:46.320 --> 01:46:48.180
To try and completely
relax in between sets,

01:46:48.180 --> 01:46:49.830
combine that with the physiological sigh,

01:46:49.830 --> 01:46:53.160
and then when the set,
the next set commences,

01:46:53.160 --> 01:46:55.470
employ that very strong grip, both, again,

01:46:55.470 --> 01:46:57.138
of the weight that's moving and the weight

01:46:57.138 --> 01:46:59.340
that at that moment might be stationary

01:46:59.340 --> 01:47:01.200
or in isometric position.

01:47:01.200 --> 01:47:03.510
So the nervous system, of course,

01:47:03.510 --> 01:47:06.570
is what controls muscles and that operates

01:47:06.570 --> 01:47:07.403
in both directions.

01:47:07.403 --> 01:47:09.870
If you want to relax,
try and use long exhales,

01:47:09.870 --> 01:47:12.480
maybe even physiological
sighs and really concentrate

01:47:12.480 --> 01:47:14.010
on mentally and physically relaxing,

01:47:14.010 --> 01:47:17.190
in particular your core and your fists.

01:47:17.190 --> 01:47:19.740
And if you want to generate force, right,

01:47:19.740 --> 01:47:22.440
you want to move a heavy
barbell or dumbbell,

01:47:22.440 --> 01:47:24.720
you want to do a chin up
with the maximal force,

01:47:24.720 --> 01:47:26.040
that's when you can employ the opposite,

01:47:26.040 --> 01:47:27.750
which would be to grip
the bar or dumbbell,

01:47:27.750 --> 01:47:29.220
et cetera, very tightly.

01:47:29.220 --> 01:47:32.460
And you want to contract
your core or even fill

01:47:32.460 --> 01:47:33.960
your body with air as a, say,

01:47:33.960 --> 01:47:35.820
plug all the leaks, et cetera.

01:47:35.820 --> 01:47:37.758
So this gets into kind
of form and movement,

01:47:37.758 --> 01:47:41.340
which is an extensive
near infinite landscape

01:47:41.340 --> 01:47:43.710
of discussion, again, that we
don't have time to go into.

01:47:43.710 --> 01:47:46.380
I just want to mention those
two nervous system related tips

01:47:46.380 --> 01:47:48.780
because I suppose as a
neuroscientist, they appeal

01:47:48.780 --> 01:47:50.430
to me because they're
grounded in fundamental

01:47:50.430 --> 01:47:52.770
principles of how the nervous
system innervates muscle.

01:47:52.770 --> 01:47:54.180
And I know that they will benefit

01:47:54.180 --> 01:47:56.430
you the first time you
use them and every time.

01:47:56.430 --> 01:47:58.380
Speaking of grip and
nervous system and fitness

01:47:58.380 --> 01:48:01.176
and longevity, Dr. Peter
Attia, who is a medical doctor,

01:48:01.176 --> 01:48:03.750
was a guest on the Huberman
Lab podcast and provided

01:48:03.750 --> 01:48:06.420
an enormous wealth of information
on that podcast episode.

01:48:06.420 --> 01:48:09.300
I really encourage you to check
it out when you have time.

01:48:09.300 --> 01:48:11.610
And of course has his
own spectacular podcast,

01:48:11.610 --> 01:48:13.290
The Drive with Peter Attia.

01:48:13.290 --> 01:48:15.723
Peter, Dr. Attia, I should say,

01:48:16.560 --> 01:48:19.597
often talks about certain
movements or exercises

01:48:19.597 --> 01:48:22.110
that you should perform not
just to improve your fitness,

01:48:22.110 --> 01:48:25.560
but also to touch into or
measure how fit you are

01:48:25.560 --> 01:48:27.777
and how well you are
progressing toward a long

01:48:27.777 --> 01:48:29.460
lifespan and healthspan.

01:48:29.460 --> 01:48:31.920
And one of those includes
the ability to hang

01:48:31.920 --> 01:48:34.080
from a bar for a minute or longer.

01:48:34.080 --> 01:48:37.680
And there are a number
of different expectations

01:48:37.680 --> 01:48:40.200
that one can have of how
long they should be able

01:48:40.200 --> 01:48:42.150
to hang from a bar depending on their age

01:48:42.150 --> 01:48:43.500
and their fitness level, et cetera.

01:48:43.500 --> 01:48:45.960
Please check out Dr. Attia's podcast

01:48:45.960 --> 01:48:48.150
and his various social
media sites to get more

01:48:48.150 --> 01:48:49.260
information on that.

01:48:49.260 --> 01:48:51.360
But what I can tell you is
that if you're going to hang

01:48:51.360 --> 01:48:54.090
from a bar and you want to
hang from that bar as long

01:48:54.090 --> 01:48:56.520
as possible, which turns
out to be a interesting

01:48:56.520 --> 01:48:59.010
and important metric of your health,

01:48:59.010 --> 01:49:01.530
then gripping the bar very
tightly will actually help.

01:49:01.530 --> 01:49:03.600
Earlier we talked about
whether or not to train

01:49:03.600 --> 01:49:06.390
if you're sleep deprived
and how to recover

01:49:06.390 --> 01:49:09.690
from what I would say is
moderate sleep deprivation

01:49:09.690 --> 01:49:12.870
by doing NSDR as opposed
to total sleep deprivation

01:49:12.870 --> 01:49:14.520
like being up all night or having a truly

01:49:14.520 --> 01:49:16.173
miserable night, which case,

01:49:16.173 --> 01:49:18.750
I think you should just
skip training the next day

01:49:18.750 --> 01:49:19.890
and slide it forward.

01:49:19.890 --> 01:49:22.740
Now, a similar issue
comes up from time to time

01:49:22.740 --> 01:49:25.440
where people wonder whether
or not they should train

01:49:25.440 --> 01:49:27.240
or not if they're sick.

01:49:27.240 --> 01:49:31.770
And here there's all
sorts of crazy gym lore

01:49:31.770 --> 01:49:33.546
and sport specific lore.

01:49:33.546 --> 01:49:36.390
For instance, I used to hear this,

01:49:36.390 --> 01:49:37.350
when I ran cross country,

01:49:37.350 --> 01:49:42.350
there was this adage
that if the symptoms were

01:49:42.780 --> 01:49:45.150
from the neck up, you could still train.

01:49:45.150 --> 01:49:46.860
That is if you were really
congested and you had

01:49:46.860 --> 01:49:48.660
a headache, you could still run.

01:49:48.660 --> 01:49:50.730
Whereas if it was in your
chest and in your lungs

01:49:50.730 --> 01:49:51.563
you couldn't run.

01:49:51.563 --> 01:49:53.820
I don't think there's any data whatsoever

01:49:53.820 --> 01:49:56.070
to support whether or not that's true

01:49:56.070 --> 01:49:58.028
or whether it's not true.

01:49:58.028 --> 01:50:03.028
For myself, and because
my general goal is to be

01:50:03.951 --> 01:50:06.330
training and fit over time,

01:50:06.330 --> 01:50:10.410
but also to include general
health in the fitness equation,

01:50:10.410 --> 01:50:12.690
that is to not be sick or chronically sick

01:50:12.690 --> 01:50:15.330
and certainly not to
get other people sick.

01:50:15.330 --> 01:50:17.970
If I have a little tiny sniffle,

01:50:17.970 --> 01:50:21.210
like I think I might be
getting sick, even then,

01:50:21.210 --> 01:50:23.610
I'm a little cautious in
the sense that I'm not going

01:50:23.610 --> 01:50:25.800
to do my typical workout.

01:50:25.800 --> 01:50:28.470
I might stop at about 15 minutes earlier.

01:50:28.470 --> 01:50:31.770
And I would do that not by
neglecting any body parts

01:50:31.770 --> 01:50:32.670
or anything of that sort.

01:50:32.670 --> 01:50:34.824
If it's a weight training workout,

01:50:34.824 --> 01:50:36.750
by simply reducing the
total number of sets,

01:50:36.750 --> 01:50:39.720
I probably wouldn't do any
sets to failure, if I did,

01:50:39.720 --> 01:50:42.210
I might reduce the total
number or percentage of sets

01:50:42.210 --> 01:50:45.360
to failure from about 30%
of sets to maybe closer

01:50:45.360 --> 01:50:47.721
to 10% of sets, something like that.

01:50:47.721 --> 01:50:49.320
And if it was endurance work,

01:50:49.320 --> 01:50:51.840
I might throttle back by 10 or 20%.

01:50:51.840 --> 01:50:54.330
And I will shorten the total
duration of the workout.

01:50:54.330 --> 01:50:58.380
And I often find that
because of the known, yes,

01:50:58.380 --> 01:51:01.260
peer reviewed known immune
system enhancing effects

01:51:01.260 --> 01:51:04.006
of exercise, sometimes
that alone will allow me

01:51:04.006 --> 01:51:06.060
to avoid getting sick.

01:51:06.060 --> 01:51:08.130
But of course I'm also
careful to get home,

01:51:08.130 --> 01:51:10.890
take a hot shower, not stress myself out,

01:51:10.890 --> 01:51:13.440
if I can avoid getting
myself stressed out and focus

01:51:13.440 --> 01:51:15.870
on sleep, NSDR, other forms of recovery,

01:51:15.870 --> 01:51:17.700
good nutrition, et cetera.

01:51:17.700 --> 01:51:19.783
If however, I have a real sniffle, a cold,

01:51:19.783 --> 01:51:22.950
I'm not feeling well or
I think I might be coming

01:51:22.950 --> 01:51:23.783
down with a flu,

01:51:23.783 --> 01:51:25.265
I absolutely do not train
and I don't get back

01:51:25.265 --> 01:51:29.730
into training of any kind
until I'm completely recovered.

01:51:29.730 --> 01:51:31.890
So what I'm basically saying is that, no,

01:51:31.890 --> 01:51:34.440
I don't believe you should
train if you're sick.

01:51:34.440 --> 01:51:37.230
And perhaps equally importantly,

01:51:37.230 --> 01:51:39.720
when you come back from
a layoff of any kind,

01:51:39.720 --> 01:51:43.470
whether or not because of
illness or for whatever reason,

01:51:43.470 --> 01:51:46.093
I do believe that because
your body is a bit untrained,

01:51:46.093 --> 01:51:48.870
it's not ideal to jump
right back into maximal

01:51:48.870 --> 01:51:50.790
training and to take one,

01:51:50.790 --> 01:51:54.270
maybe two weeks of ramping
up to the full duration

01:51:54.270 --> 01:51:56.730
and intensity of workouts
that then I would continue on

01:51:56.730 --> 01:52:01.110
going for however many
cycles I can complete

01:52:01.110 --> 01:52:05.310
before I hit another
sickness or I hit another gap

01:52:05.310 --> 01:52:07.710
in my schedule due to family obligations

01:52:07.710 --> 01:52:09.090
or other obligations, et cetera.

01:52:09.090 --> 01:52:11.430
So we've covered a lot
of tools and protocols

01:52:11.430 --> 01:52:13.650
and variables related to fitness,

01:52:13.650 --> 01:52:16.110
but we have by no means
covered all the available

01:52:16.110 --> 01:52:17.810
tools and protocols and variables.

01:52:18.750 --> 01:52:21.750
Before we wrap up, I do
want to emphasize one tool.

01:52:21.750 --> 01:52:24.660
It's a very easy, in fact, zero cost,

01:52:24.660 --> 01:52:27.210
very low time commitment tool.

01:52:27.210 --> 01:52:29.160
And this was one that was provided, again,

01:52:29.160 --> 01:52:32.610
by Dr. Andy Galpin when he was
on the Huberman Lab podcast.

01:52:32.610 --> 01:52:35.130
And it's a tool that there
is excellent research

01:52:35.130 --> 01:52:37.800
to support the effectiveness of,

01:52:37.800 --> 01:52:40.350
and that I do believe
should come at the end

01:52:40.350 --> 01:52:42.090
of every training session.

01:52:42.090 --> 01:52:45.030
And that's to do three to five minutes

01:52:45.030 --> 01:52:47.670
of deliberately slowed breathing.

01:52:47.670 --> 01:52:49.050
It sounds so simple,

01:52:49.050 --> 01:52:52.320
three to five minutes of
deliberately slowed breathing.

01:52:52.320 --> 01:52:54.300
So this could be while
you're in the shower

01:52:54.300 --> 01:52:56.040
or when you arrive at your car,

01:52:56.040 --> 01:52:57.600
you might sit in your car quietly and do

01:52:57.600 --> 01:52:58.830
that if you have time or maybe

01:52:58.830 --> 01:53:01.380
even while you're driving back to,

01:53:01.380 --> 01:53:04.710
or onto your next destination,

01:53:04.710 --> 01:53:06.840
just to really slow down your breathing,

01:53:06.840 --> 01:53:09.930
to really look at the
recovery period that has

01:53:09.930 --> 01:53:11.730
to follow each training session.

01:53:11.730 --> 01:53:13.650
And of course, during
which the adaptations,

01:53:13.650 --> 01:53:16.320
the changes that make you
more fit than you were

01:53:16.320 --> 01:53:18.960
going into the exercise occur.

01:53:18.960 --> 01:53:21.150
And that three to five
minutes of deliberately

01:53:21.150 --> 01:53:23.880
slowed breathing has been
shown in Andy's group

01:53:23.880 --> 01:53:26.010
and in related experiments,

01:53:26.010 --> 01:53:27.960
not exactly the same,
but related experiments

01:53:27.960 --> 01:53:30.120
in our laboratory, in other laboratories,

01:53:30.120 --> 01:53:32.730
to really so-called
downshift the nervous system

01:53:32.730 --> 01:53:37.200
and really set you up for
maximal recovery, rapid recovery,

01:53:37.200 --> 01:53:39.570
and allow you to lean into
the next training session

01:53:39.570 --> 01:53:40.590
with full intensity

01:53:40.590 --> 01:53:42.510
when that training session
eventually arrives.

01:53:42.510 --> 01:53:44.010
So it's a very simple tool,

01:53:44.010 --> 01:53:47.400
but a very potent tool
for your overall fitness.

01:53:47.400 --> 01:53:49.350
So thank you for joining
me for this discussion

01:53:49.350 --> 01:53:52.560
of what I'm calling a
foundational, or yes,

01:53:52.560 --> 01:53:53.910
we could even get bold and call

01:53:53.910 --> 01:53:55.380
it an optimal fitness protocol.

01:53:55.380 --> 01:53:57.870
Although the word optimal is a tricky one.

01:53:57.870 --> 01:54:00.000
There's no real optimal fitness protocol.

01:54:00.000 --> 01:54:02.010
And today what I've
really tried to focus on

01:54:02.010 --> 01:54:05.700
is this foundational protocol
because it does allow

01:54:05.700 --> 01:54:07.560
you to check off most,

01:54:07.560 --> 01:54:12.420
if not all the boxes related
to strength, endurance,

01:54:12.420 --> 01:54:17.420
hypertrophy, speed, power, flexibility.

01:54:17.760 --> 01:54:19.500
It will also teach you how
to regulate your nervous

01:54:19.500 --> 01:54:20.670
system up and down.

01:54:20.670 --> 01:54:24.630
That is to ramp up and focus,
mind muscle link, et cetera,

01:54:24.630 --> 01:54:27.240
and then quickly calm
down, physiological sighs,

01:54:27.240 --> 01:54:28.770
three to five minute decompress breathing

01:54:28.770 --> 01:54:30.540
at the end of training, et cetera.

01:54:30.540 --> 01:54:32.520
Really, even though I
talked about the protocol

01:54:32.520 --> 01:54:34.050
that I follow, and again,

01:54:34.050 --> 01:54:36.979
that we will provide as a
newsletter at hubermanlab.com

01:54:36.979 --> 01:54:39.360
if you want to look at it in more detail,

01:54:39.360 --> 01:54:42.150
even though we talked about it
in the context of what I do,

01:54:42.150 --> 01:54:45.360
again, I really want to
emphasize that this protocol

01:54:45.360 --> 01:54:47.430
and the description of
this protocol and all

01:54:47.430 --> 01:54:50.640
its variables is really for
you and for you to tailor

01:54:50.640 --> 01:54:51.810
to your specific needs.

01:54:51.810 --> 01:54:55.380
So please, take the
protocol into consideration,

01:54:55.380 --> 01:54:56.670
